(function (root) { root.STEEL_GLOSSARY_EXTRA = [{"n":1001,"term":"Units of stress: kips or pounds over square inches give ksi or psi","topic":"Stress-strain curve","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what are the units of stress","stress units","kips divided by square inches","load divided by area units","ksi","psi","pounds per square inch","kips per square inch","units of load over area","what unit is stress in"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"So if I take a load in pounds or kips and I divided by the area, what are my units?","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"You can definitely pass statics and steel without really understanding units, but when you get to concrete, it's going to be a problem.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Stress is load divided by area, so a load in pounds or kips over an area in square inches gives pounds per square inch (psi) or kips per square inch (ksi). The caption after her question is garbled ('ASI. Pounds per square. Inch or pounds per square foot?'); the best reading is ksi or psi, because the area is in square inches. She said you can pass statics and steel without really understanding units, but concrete (next semester) will make unit sense a problem.","answers":["A load in kips divided by an area in square inches gives a stress in what unit?","What are the units of stress in this course?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md section 2A 'Stress units' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1002,"term":"Statics used part of the stress-strain curve; steel design uses all of it","topic":"Stress-strain curve","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["what part of the stress strain curve did statics use","statics versus steel design stress strain curve","entire stress strain curve","why do we use the entire curve","we limited our conversation in statics","statics stopped at first yield","how much of the curve does this course use","go through the entire range","yield strain hardening ultimate failure range"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"In statics we limited our conversation because we were learning about stress and strains. This semester we are going to go through the entire range. We are going to be using the entire stress strength curve.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"It's to show you we stopped last semester. So we were in a very limited part of the stress strain curve to capture additional capacity.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"In statics the conversation was limited to the early part of the stress-strain curve (stress and strain, below the elastic limit). In this course she goes through the entire range (yield strength, hardening, ultimate strength, failure) and uses the entire stress-strain curve; she said the Steel Manual has a picture of it. On 9/23 she said it again for beams: statics stopped in a very limited part of the curve, and design now captures the additional capacity.","answers":["Which part of the stress-strain curve did statics use, and which part does this course use?","Why does steel design use the whole stress-strain curve?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Statics versus this course'; B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-176; classes 2026-08-26 and 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1003,"term":"Why a high-carbon steel is not used where the stresses reverse (wind bracing)","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why not use high carbon steel for wind bracing","high carbon steel brittle","stress reversal brittle steel","wind bracing brittle steel","why would you not use carbon steel","flaw in brittle steel","stresses reverse themselves","carbon makes steel brittle where stresses reverse","hard steel snaps","brittle steel breaks suddenly"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"So you wouldn't want to use it in a circumstance where you have issues. Where the stresses reverse themselves. You might use it and it's just a flaw. You wouldn't use it for wind bracing. Because it's brittle.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"More carbon gives hardness and strength but makes steel more brittle. Where the stresses reverse themselves, as in wind bracing, a flaw in a brittle steel could start a failure, so she would not use it there. The caption wording is rough ('You might use it and it's just a flaw'); this is the reader's best reading.","answers":["Why would a high-carbon steel not be used for wind bracing?","What is the problem with using a brittle steel where the stresses reverse?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'High carbon and reversing stress' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1004,"term":"What the designer specifies and what the fabricator supplies","topic":"Construction and fire","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["what does the designer give the fabricator","what does the fabricator supply","designer versus fabricator","specify the strength and size","steel fabricators","who supplies the different pieces","why do designers need the history of steel","role of the fabricator"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"We are just going to specify the strength and size and they will supply the different pieces.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"This was actually not that important to us as designers except to understand what our steel fabricators are telling us.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"The designer only specifies the strength and size of the steel; the fabricator supplies the different pieces. She said the history of steel (cast iron, wrought iron, mild, medium and high carbon steel) is not that important to designers except to understand what their steel fabricators are telling them.","answers":["What does the designer specify and what does the fabricator supply?","Why does a designer need to know a little of the history of steel?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Designer versus fabricator' (NO); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1005,"term":"Why one Manual covers every shape (mills used to have their own)","topic":"The Manual","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why can designers use one manual for every shape","every steel mill had its own steel manual","mill manuals","early 1900s steel manual","shapes standardized internationally","why are the shapes standardized","which mill to buy steel from","dimensions chapter standardized"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"It use d to be way back in the early 1900s that every steel mill had it's own steel manual. So you had to know where you were going to get the steel. But today, even internationally it's all standardized.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"In the early 1900s every steel mill had its own steel manual, so you had to know where you were going to get the steel. Today the shapes are standardized, even internationally, and they are all in the Steel Manual in the chapter called Dimensions (Part 1). The caption has a stray 'It use d' (= It used).","answers":["Why can a designer today use one Manual for every shape?","What was different about steel manuals in the early 1900s?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Mill manuals' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1006,"term":"Floor-to-ceiling space limits the depth of the structure","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why is the depth of a member important","what must fit between the finished floor and the finished ceiling","floor to ceiling space","how much room is there between floor and ceiling","ceiling height 8 10 12 feet","code limit on building height","structure and mechanical in the same space","why does depth matter in design"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"You must realize that between the finished floor and the finished ceiling, there's only so much room and a lot of stuff has to go on here.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"you can only build so tall. Those are code issues and you want to have X number of floors and you want to have the ceiling at a certain height. 8, 10, 12 feet.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Between the finished floor and the finished ceiling there is only so much room, and the structure of the floor, the structural framing, the mechanical systems and everything else must fit in it. How much room there is comes from the code limit on how tall you can build, the number of floors you want and the ceiling height you want (8, 10 or 12 ft). That is why the depth of a member is one of the two most important facts about it (the other is its weight, which is cost).","answers":["What has to fit in the space between the finished floor and the finished ceiling?","What fixes how much room there is for the structure of a floor?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Floor-to-ceiling space' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1007,"term":"The lightest section is not always the best solution","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"reason","synonyms":["is the lightest section always the best solution","most economical is not always the best","when not to choose the lightest","why not always pick the lightest beam","very good reason to pick something that isn't economical","the best solution isn't the most economical","cheapest is not always the best","weight is an easy way to compare"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Doesn't mean that's the best solution for you. Sometimes the best solution isn't the most economical, but an easy way to compare it is to look at the weight and the second set of numbers.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"We are not looking for the one that's closest. We are looking for the lightest. Unless there's a very good reason to pick something that isn't economical.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"Weight is an easy way to compare options (it takes out length and fabrication) and the lighter section is the cheaper one, but that does not mean it is the best solution for the project: sometimes the best solution is not the most economical. On 9/30 she said to choose the lightest, not the closest, unless there is a very good reason to pick something that is not economical.","answers":["True or false: the lightest section is always the best solution.","When might you pick a section that is not the most economical?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Lightest is not always best' (PARTLY); C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md G7 and inventory #13; classes 2026-08-26 and 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1008,"term":"Tabulated thicknesses are averages: use the tabulated thickness (captions unclear)","topic":"The Manual","kind":"fact","synonyms":["are the thicknesses in the manual averages","tabulated thickness","which thickness do i use","thickness is an average","edges of the angle thickness","use the thickness in the property tables","flange thickness average","thickness in the shape tables"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"When we talk about thickness and look at thicknesses you have to remember they were giving us an average, the edges of the angle occur. But we're going to use the thickness.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"She said the thicknesses listed are an average (the edges, for example of an angle, vary), but that the tabulated thickness is the one to use. The captions are unclear here ('the edges of the angle occur'); it is not certain whether she meant angle legs or flanges.","answers":["The tables give thicknesses that are averages because the edges vary; which value do you use in calculations?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Tabulated thickness' (PARTLY) and DOUBTS 4A; class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1009,"term":"Why steel's strength can be trusted more than concrete's or wood's","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["why is steel a consistent material","concrete is like cooking","steel comes with paperwork","steel from a mill certificate","wood comes from a tree flaws","why can you trust the strength of steel","concrete mixture sand gravel water cement unknown","steel versus concrete versus wood consistency"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"When we specify steel, size and strength that comes from a steel mill you get paperwork. We know what we are getting.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"When you're talking about concrete, it's like cooking. You have some sand, gravel, water, cement. I don't know what the mixture is.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"Wood comes from a tree; there are still flaws in it because it comes from a tree.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Steel is specified by size and strength and comes from a steel mill with paperwork, so you know what you are getting and can have high confidence in the Manual. Concrete is like cooking (some sand, gravel, water and cement; she does not know the mixture, 'too many cooks in the kitchen'), which creates potential problems. Wood still has flaws because it comes from a tree. Steel's properties are uniform and do not change over time.","answers":["Why can a designer trust the strength of steel more than that of concrete or wood?","What did she compare concrete to when explaining that steel is a consistent material?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Steel versus concrete and wood quality' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1010,"term":"Concrete is the biggest place for screw-ups; steel mills are reliable","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"fact","synonyms":["which material is the biggest place for screw ups","is structural steel reliable","has structural steel ever underperformed","pre-engineered wood reliable","concrete is like making muffins","concrete is made on site","steel comes with a certificate","steel mills are very reliable today"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"I don't know of a case where structural steel underperformed.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"Preengineered wood also reliable. Biggest place for screwups is in concrete because it's like cooking. Like making Muffins.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"It's a man made product and being made on the site.","date":"2026-09-09"}],"summary":"Asked whether steel is reliable, she said she knows of no case where structural steel underperformed: steel mills are very reliable today, what is in the Manual is what you get, and steel comes from a mill with a certificate. Pre-engineered wood is also reliable. The biggest place for screw-ups is concrete, because it is a man-made product made on the site, like cooking or making muffins (crushed rock, sand, water, cement, following a recipe).","answers":["Which material did she call the biggest place for screw-ups, and why?","Besides steel, which material did she call reliable?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md items B-099, B-100, B-101; class 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1011,"term":"Why bad soil argues against a concrete structure: concrete is very heavy","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why would bad soil argue against concrete","bad soil conditions","concrete is very heavy","most of the work a concrete structure does is hold itself up","steel is light for its strength","poor soil steel or concrete","soil conditions and structure weight","why not build in concrete on bad soil"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Concrete is very heavy. If you got bad soil conditions, you're not building with concrete. Because most of the work concrete structure does is hold itself up. Steel, considering how strong it is, it's very light.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Concrete is very heavy, and most of the work a concrete structure does is hold itself up, so if you have bad soil conditions you are not building with concrete. Steel, considering how strong it is, is very light (she meant regular structural steel, not 'lightweight'). This is the consequence of steel's high strength-to-weight ratio.","answers":["Why would bad soil conditions argue against building in concrete?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Bad soil' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1012,"term":"Cost as a disadvantage of steel: exotic where there is no steel industry","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"reason","synonyms":["cost disadvantage of steel","why is steel costly","steel industry or low cost steel","exotic for where you're building","is steel always expensive","cost is a disadvantage of steel","disadvantages of steel cost","steel expensive in some places"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"I guess if you have a steel industry or can get low cost steel, but let's put it down any way. It's exotic for where you're building; yes.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"A student named cost as a disadvantage. She accepted it with a condition: steel is not costly if you have a steel industry or can get low-cost steel, but it is exotic (and so costly) for some places where you are building. Cost is not on her written list of disadvantages (painting, rust, fireproofing, buckling, fatigue, delivery).","answers":["Besides fireproofing, what cost problem can steel have?","When is steel not a costly choice?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Cost disadvantage' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1013,"term":"What rust does to a brick building on a steel frame","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what does rusting steel do to brick","rust pushes up on the brick","cracks in old brick town houses","brick buildings on steel frames rust","why do brick walls crack over a rusting steel frame","rust grows thick","new york brick buildings steel rust","rust scale pushes the masonry"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"In New York City, where we have all of these lovely brick buildings, is sitting on a steel, and it will happen to rust over time. It gets very thick and pushes up on the grid.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"Because when you look at these town houses and you see these cracks, this was why. It pushes up and brick like concrete it's very brittle.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"In New York City many of the lovely brick buildings sit on a steel frame that rusts over time. The rust gets very thick and pushes up (the caption says 'on the grid', probably the brick or masonry); the brick is brittle, like concrete, and cracks, which is why you see these cracks in town houses. Her slide gives the figure: rust scale can be 12 times the original thickness.","answers":["What does rusting steel do to a brick building on a steel frame?","Why do old New York town houses show cracks in the brick?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Rust anecdote' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1014,"term":"The code is the minimum: why a designer may give more than the two-hour fire rating","topic":"Construction and fire","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why provide more than the code requires","the code is the least you can do","100 story building evacuate","responsible designer judgment beyond the code","two hour fire rating is a minimum","is the code a maximum or a minimum","use judgment in addition to the code","fire rating more time to evacuate"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"The code is the least you can do. If you know as a responsible designer that you need more time do better than that; not less. We have to use our judgment in addition to the code.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"The code asks for a two-hour fire rating for office buildings, meaning two hours to evacuate, but she said the code is the least you can do. In a 100-story building everybody tries to get out at the same time; a responsible designer who knows more time is needed does better than the code, not less, and uses judgment in addition to the code.","answers":["The code requires a two-hour fire rating; why might a designer provide more?","Is the building code a maximum or a minimum?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Code as the minimum' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1015,"term":"Steel building erection sequence in her video","topic":"Construction and fire","kind":"fact","synonyms":["how is a steel building put up","foundations of a steel building are concrete","columns pop up above the floor","next level columns sit on top of the lower ones","steel frame erection sequence","beams frame to the girders","video of a steel building going up","why do the columns stick up above the floor"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Here comes the next level columns that sits on top of the lower one.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"From the video she showed: the foundations are concrete; the columns are set, then the beams between the columns; the columns always pop up above the floor so the next level's columns can sit on top of the lower ones (she said how this affects design comes when columns are covered); beams frame to the girders and the load comes down. The captions of this passage are garbled ('Mary list columns', 'herders' = girders).","answers":["What material are the foundations of a steel-framed building, and what do the columns do at each floor?","How does the next level's column connect to the one below?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Steel building sequence, video' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1016,"term":"Floor and cladding on a steel frame (her video)","topic":"Construction and fire","kind":"fact","synonyms":["how is the floor formed on a steel frame","metal deck with concrete on top","deck spans between the beams","cladding hangs off the framing","how is the cladding carried","what carries the facade of a steel building","building skin on a steel frame","metal deck concrete floor"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"This is the metal deck. Maybe you're pouring concrete on top of this to make the floor. That's spanning between the beams.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"And then last, but not least you scale the building with whatever your materials are that's also hanging off the framing.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"In the same video: a metal deck sits on the beams and concrete may be poured on top of it to make the floor, spanning between the beams. Last, the building is scaled (clad) with whatever materials are chosen, and the cladding hangs off the framing.","answers":["How is the floor formed on the beams of a steel frame, and how is the cladding carried?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Floor and cladding, video' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1017,"term":"A lighter replacement of a finish is still dead load","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"fact","synonyms":["is a lighter finish still dead load","replace a finish with something lighter dead or live load","peel away and put in something lighter","dead load there all the time lighter material","is the new floor finish dead load or live load","swapping a finish for a lighter one","a lighter material is still dead load"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"We can peel away and put in something lighter, but it still will be considered dead load because it's there all the time.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Dead load is anything that is there all the time. If you peel away a finish and put in something lighter, it is still considered dead load, because it is there all the time.","answers":["A heavy finish is replaced by a lighter one; is the new finish dead load or live load?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'A lighter replacement is still dead load' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1018,"term":"Where dead loads are found: the architectural sections you draw","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"fact","synonyms":["where do you find the dead loads","architectural sections dead load","sections you draw show the dead load","3/4 inch marble wood ceiling","how does the consultant calculate the dead load","which drawing shows the dead load of a floor","dead loads come from the section","dead load from the architect's section"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"In the architectural sections. One of those sections you draw. It tells your consultant what you're thinking, 3 quart inch marble, wood, ceiling.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"The way you're consulting will be able to calculate the load is through these sections.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"The dead loads are found in the architectural sections you draw: they show, for example, 3/4 inch marble, wood and ceiling, which tells your consultant what you are thinking, and the consultant calculates the load from those sections. (Caption garbles: '3 quart inch' = 3/4 inch; 'consulting' = consultant.)","answers":["On which drawings do you find the dead loads of a floor?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Where dead loads are found' (NO); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1019,"term":"Live loads come from the building code (same in NYC, BOCA, IBC)","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"fact","synonyms":["where do live loads come from","do live loads differ from city to city","live loads are the same in the new york boca and international codes","live loads from the building code","boca code","international building code live load","new york city code live load","typical live loads don't change"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"They don't change. It is the same numbers in the New York City code. Boca code. International building codes.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Live loads come from the building code, because they depend on use and occupancy. The typical live-load numbers do not change: they are the same in the New York City code, the BOCA code and the International building codes (house 40, office 50, gymnasium 100 psf).","answers":["Where do live loads come from, and do the numbers differ from city to city?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Source of live loads' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1020,"term":"Wind or earthquake: which lateral load governs in New York City and in California","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["which lateral load governs in new york city","wind or earthquake in new york","california earthquake governs","wind always governs in new york","seismic or wind new york versus california","lateral loads depend on geography","what drives the lateral loads","design for wind in new york"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"but wind always governs. In other words, it was more load on our structure. We always designing for wind. You go to California it's reverse.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Lateral loads depend on where the building is. In New York City there is an earthquake (seismic) code but wind always governs, because it puts more load on the structure, so you always design for wind; in California it is the reverse. The wind and seismic numbers are the drivers of the lateral loads. (Caption garbles: 'assignment code' and 'sizement' = seismic.)","answers":["Which lateral load governs in New York City, and which in California?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Wind or earthquake' (NO); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1021,"term":"How lateral loads vary: wind with height, soil with depth","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"fact","synonyms":["how does wind load change with height","soil pressure increases with depth","load on a basement wall","wind increases as we go up","the balcony on the 8th floor is windier","triangular load approximated by a point load","wind load versus soil load variation","why is soil load triangular"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"as we go up, the wind increases. We are on the 8th floor on the balcony, it feels a lot windier.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"As we go deeper, there's more push on the wall. We might approximate some point load instead of the triangular load the idea is the same.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Wind increases as you go up (an 8th-floor balcony feels a lot windier). Soil is the opposite: on a basement wall there is more push the deeper you go, a triangular load that might be approximated by a point load; the idea is the same.","answers":["How does wind load change with height, and how does soil pressure on a basement wall change with depth?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Variation of lateral loads' (NO); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1022,"term":"Renovating a building: work backwards to the capacity","topic":"Analysis and design","kind":"fact","synonyms":["renovating a building change of use how do you check it","residence to restaurant 40 to 100 psf","repurposing a building","work backwards to find the capacity","renovation capacity versus the new use","you know the geometric property because the structure is there","which two of the three do you know in a renovation"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"You know the geometric property because the structure is there. You know the material because the structure is there.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"You're work ing backwards to see what is the capacity and compare it to what you're going to be using it for.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"When renovating or repurposing a building (her example: a residence at 40 psf becoming a restaurant at 100 psf) you do not choose the geometric property or the material, because the structure is already there. You know those two and work backwards to find the capacity, then compare it with what the building will now be used for.","answers":["A residence designed for 40 psf becomes a restaurant at 100 psf; how do you check the structure?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Renovation example' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1023,"term":"The concrete designers changed to ultimate-strength design in 1972 (as she remembers)","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"fact","synonyms":["when did concrete designers change to ultimate strength design","1972 ultimate strength design","the concrete people changed","which designers changed to ultimate strength design first","history of ultimate strength design","concrete ultimate strength design year"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"The concrete people changed ultimate strength design in 1972; I think.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"She said the concrete people changed to ultimate-strength design in 1972, and added 'I think' (the captions print the digits garbled; the reader's best reading is the 1970s).","answers":["Which group of designers changed to ultimate-strength design first, and about when?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Concrete and ultimate-strength design' (NO); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1024,"term":"Allowable or ultimate: the one word that decides the overload factors (anchor)","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"reason","synonyms":["how do you know whether to apply load factors to a catalog value","anchor pull out value allowable or ultimate","allowable or ultimate in a manufacturer's chart","multi anchor catalog","the one word that tells you","do i apply overload factors to a catalog value","they are not going to explain it to you","ten kips anchor pull out"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"I apply overload factors or not is that one word does it say allowable or ultimate. They are not going to explain it to you.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Manufacturers' charts give either allowable strength or ultimate strength. Her anchor example: an anchor with a pull-out value of ten kips. The only thing that tells you whether to apply the overload factors to your loads is that one word, allowable or ultimate: an allowable value already has the safety factor in it, while an ultimate value means you have to increase (factor) your loads. 'They are not going to explain it to you.'","answers":["A catalog gives an anchor pull-out value of 10 kips; what decides whether you apply the overload factors to your loads?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Anchor catalog' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1025,"term":"Why the dead-load factor is 0.9 with wind or earthquake","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why is the dead load factor 0.9","why 0.9 on dead load","0.9d with wind","overturning and stabilizing","dead weight stabilizes the building","we overestimate dead load","why is one load factor less than 1","wind times lever arm against dead weight times lever arm"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"It's trying to turn this building clockwise. To turn it over it's toe. One stabilizes it.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"I would be applying the wind load times this lever arm and what's keeping it from flipping over is the dead weight times this.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"If I overestimate by dead load, which we all tend to do, then I'm assuming I have more stability than I have.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"All the load factors are one or greater except the factor on dead load in the combinations with a lateral load (0.9 D). A lateral load such as wind or earthquake tries to turn the building over about its toe, and the dead weight is what keeps it from flipping (wind load times its lever arm against dead weight times its lever arm). People tend to overestimate dead load, which would overstate the stability, so in that case dead load is reduced instead of increased. (Caption garbles: 'sizement' = seismic; 'any accounts to dead load' = the factor on dead load.)","answers":["Why is the dead-load factor only 0.9 in the combinations with wind or earthquake?","Why is it unsafe to overestimate dead load when there is a lateral load?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Reason for 0.9 on dead load' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1026,"term":"Drawings give the bolt diameter (the shaft), not the hole diameter","topic":"Bolts and connections","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why do drawings give the bolt diameter","bolt size on drawings","bolt diameter versus hole diameter","a drawing gives 3/4 inch bolts what hole size","diameter of the shaft gives the bolt strength","which diameter is given on a drawing","bolt diameter given hole diameter computed","3/4 inch bolt 7/8 inch hole"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"any drawing you look at they are going to give you the bolt diameter. That's what's given. Because the diameter of the shaft is what generates how much we can calculate the strength of the bolt tension.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"Any drawing gives the bolt diameter, the diameter of the shaft, because the shaft diameter is what sets the strength of the bolt (the caption says 'bolt tension'). The hole you deduct in the net area is larger: bolt diameter plus 1/8 in (a 3/4 in bolt gives a 7/8 in hole).","answers":["A drawing gives 3/4 in bolts; what hole size do you use, and why does the drawing give the bolt size?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Bolt size on drawings' (PARTLY); class 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1027,"term":"Why bolts are sometimes staggered: a narrow member would become like Swiss cheese","topic":"Tension members","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why are bolts offset","why stagger the bolts","swiss cheese","member too narrow for all the bolts in one line","distance between bolts and the edge too small","why not put all the bolts in one row","bolts offset in a narrow member","minimum spacing comes later"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"you can see that if this becomes too much like swiss cheese. And this length or this one, the distance between both bolts on the edge is too small.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"When a member is too narrow to take the bolts it needs in one line, the member becomes too much like Swiss cheese and the distances between the bolts and to the edge get too small, so the bolts are sometimes forced to offset (stagger). Minimum spacing and the capacity of the bolts come later in the course. The captions are rough.","answers":["Why are bolts sometimes offset instead of placed in one line?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Staggering the bolts' (NO); class 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1028,"term":"Rupture is a word for tension members: 'a fancy way of saying ripping'","topic":"Tension members","kind":"definition","synonyms":["to what kind of member does the word rupture belong","rupture is a fancy way of saying ripping","rupture is limited to tension members","what does rupture mean","rupture means ripping","is rupture a compression term","which members rupture","tear","fracture","rip","snap"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"The world rupture is really limited to tension members.","date":"2026-09-02"},{"quote":"Which is a fancy way of saying ripping.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"She said the word rupture is really limited to tension members (rupture across the reduced, net area), and called rupture 'a fancy way of saying ripping'. The caption prints 'world' for 'word'.","answers":["To what kind of member does the word rupture really belong?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Rupture' (NO); class 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1029,"term":"Why wide flanges use A992 (stronger) while plate is commonly A36","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why is a992 used for wide flanges","why use stronger steel","bottom stories generate a lot of load","a36 for plate a992 for wide flange","why stronger steel in the lower stories","a992 is stronger than a36","why different grades for plate and wide flange"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Over time A 36 is a common place for plate, but for white flange s, we use A 999. It's stronger. You use stronger steel because when you get to the bottom storage your generating a lot of load.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"A36 (Fy 36) is a common steel for plate, but for wide flanges she uses A992 (Fy 50, Fu 65), which is stronger. You use stronger steel because when you get to the bottom stories you are generating a lot of load. (Caption garbles: 'A 999' = A992; 'white flange s' = wide flanges; 'bottom storage' = bottom stories.)","answers":["Why use A992 for wide flanges when plate is commonly A36?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Stronger grade for wide flanges' (PARTLY); class 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1030,"term":"A WT chord is spliced with a connector plate; the force acts at the center of gravity","topic":"Tension members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["why does a wt chord need a connector plate","wt tension member splice","members arrive in pieces and are assembled","plate carries the tension across the joint","where does the force act in a tension member","force at the center of gravity wt","tension force pulling to the left and to the right"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"They are going to come in pieces and you're going to have to assemble them. It might come in two pieces and we use this connect or plate to get the force from one side of the joint","date":"2026-09-02"},{"quote":"Where is this force? At the center of gravity.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"Her WT lower-chord example: the members come in pieces and have to be assembled, so a connector plate carries the tension force across the joint (pulling to the left on one side and to the right on the other). The force acts at the center of gravity, which could be somewhere off the plate. The captions are rough.","answers":["Where does the force act in a tension member, and why does a WT chord need a connector plate?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Splice and centroid' (NO); class 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1031,"term":"Shear lag picture: one third of the force enters at each of three sets of bolts","topic":"Tension members","kind":"reason","synonyms":["explain shear lag with three rows of bolts","one third of the force goes through each set of bolts","why is u less than one","the force takes time to get into the member","why does bolting only the flange reduce u","lag of the force","engage the entire area","100 percent of the force into the top of the wt"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"You're pulling the top plate to the right. One third of the force goes through in sheer when it hits the first set of bolts. Another third goes throughout second set of bolts and then the third.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"Pulling a plate on the flange of a WT: one third of the force goes through in shear when it hits the first set of bolts, another third at the second set and the last third at the third set. It takes some time before 100 percent of the force is in the top of the WT and engages the entire area. U is the reduction factor that models this lag, and how much the lag costs depends on the geometry of the section.","answers":["Using three rows of bolts, explain why U is below 1 when only the flange is bolted."],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Shear lag picture' (PARTLY); class 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1032,"term":"Why steel members are compared by weight (chopped meat)","topic":"Shape names","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why do we compare solutions by weight","steel is bought like chopped meat","dollars per pound of steel","why is weight important in the shape name","stored in nebraska or times square","labor and geography taken out leaves weight","weight is cost","compare by weight not by cost"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"We buy structural steel the way we buy chopped meet. So many dollars per pound.","date":"2026-09-02"},{"quote":"it doesn't matter if the element is being stored in Nebraska or Times Square. Take out labor costs and go graphical cost and you just go by weight.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"The weight in the shape name matters because it is cost: structural steel is bought the way chopped meat is, so many dollars per pound. When you compare solutions you pick the most economical, so you compare weight, because then it does not matter where the piece is stored (Nebraska or Times Square): take out labor and geographic cost and you just go by weight. (Caption garbles: 'chopped meet'; 'graphical cost' = geographical cost.)","answers":["Why do we compare solutions by weight?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Why compare by weight' (PARTLY); class 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1033,"term":"Wood still uses allowable stress design (steel and concrete do not)","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"fact","synonyms":["which material still uses allowable stress design","wood allowable stress design","does it matter where the safety factor is","steel and concrete use ultimate strength design","reliable design for wood","wood design method","which materials use asd"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"It doesn't matter where the safety factor is. As you go through your professional life, sometimes it's on the other. Even today when we're using ultimate strength design with steel and concrete, we still use reliable design for wood.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"She said it does not matter where the safety factor sits, because through your professional life you will meet both: even today, when steel and concrete use ultimate-strength design, wood is still designed the other way. The caption says 'reliable design for wood', best read as allowable stress design.","answers":["Which material is still designed with allowable stress design while steel and concrete use ultimate-strength design?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Wood and allowable stress' (PARTLY); class 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1034,"term":"The design project: what the building is","topic":"Analysis and design","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what is the design project","the dreaded design project","term project building","two framed levels of steel on one framed level of concrete","different bay size for each student","30 by 20 ft bay","base size","a big fat homework problem"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"It's really just a big fat homework problem.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"So what I will be handing out is a very boring building. It'll be two framed levels of steel sitting on one framed level of concrete.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"The design project (she called it 'the dreaded design project' and 'a big fat homework problem') is a very boring building: two framed levels of steel sitting on one framed level of concrete. Everybody gets a different base size (for example a 30 by 20 ft or a 30 by 40 ft bay), so the work is the same but the numbers differ, and everybody hands in his or her own work. She hands out paper copies in week 9 and goes over each step with her own base size. (Captions call it 'the midterm project' once; the caption 'side' means size.)","answers":["Describe the building of the design project.","What is different from student to student in the design project?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2B 'The design project' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1035,"term":"Purpose of the design project: one decision affects other decisions","topic":"Analysis and design","kind":"reason","synonyms":["what is the purpose of the design project","what does the design project teach","one decision affects other decisions","how you space the beams has an impact","not siloed","why a design project instead of homework","design project versus studio","homework problems given this find that"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"And the purpose is to help you understand something that you are probably learning in the studio. That one decision effects other decisions.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"You're given this, now find that. How you space the beams is going to have an impact. So it's a way to have you do exactly what you've done for homework, but not siloized.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"The purpose of the design project is to help you understand what you are probably learning in studio: one decision affects other decisions (how you space the beams will have an impact on everything else). The textbook homework problems say 'you're given this, now find that'; the project has you do exactly that work but not siloed.","answers":["What does the design project teach?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2B 'Purpose of the design project' (PARTLY); class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1036,"term":"Steel in tension forgives imperfections: pulling on it makes them disappear","topic":"Tension members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["why is steel in tension forgiving of imperfections","imperfections in the steel tension","pulling makes imperfections disappear","steel in tension straightens out flaws","the nice thing about tension","what happens to imperfections in a tension member","assuming you don't poke a hole","tension versus compression imperfections"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"The nice thing about tension and steel; assuming you don't poke a hole if there are any imperfections in the steel, pulling on it kind of makes them disappear.","date":"2026-09-09"}],"summary":"The nice thing about tension in steel (as long as you do not poke a hole in it): if there are any imperfections in the steel, pulling on it 'kind of makes them disappear'. Compression is different; it has two failure modes, crushing (the strength of the steel is exhausted) and buckling (the member becomes geometrically unstable).","answers":["Why is steel in tension forgiving of imperfections when there are no holes?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-031 (NO); class 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1037,"term":"The subscript cr means critical (Pcr, Fcr): wherever E is hidden in an equation","topic":"Compression members","kind":"definition","synonyms":["what does the subscript cr stand for","cr means critical","pcr critical load","fcr critical stress","why is it called critical buckling stress","e hidden in an equation","what does cr mean in fcr","critical buckling load"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Anytime there's E hidden in an equation our CR for critical.","date":"2026-09-09"}],"summary":"The subscript cr stands for critical: any time E is hidden in an equation, the load or stress in it is a critical (buckling) one, as in Pcr and Fcr. (Her words are rough: 'our CR for critical'.)","answers":["What does the subscript cr in Fcr and Pcr stand for?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-037 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1038,"term":"Fixed-fixed end condition: her example is a piece of steel embedded in concrete","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["give a physical example of a fixed support for a column","fixed fixed column example","steel embedded in concrete","where is a fixed support","we know right where the supports are","what is a fixed fixed column","fixed end example","embedded in concrete fixed end"],"not":[],"definitions":[],"summary":"SUMMARY (not her words; no clear caption or slide line to quote): Her example of a fixed support for a column (fixed-fixed): a piece of steel embedded in concrete, where we know right where the supports are. The caption reads 'A peace of industrial imbedded in concrete'; the reader's best reading is a piece of steel.","answers":["Give a physical example of a fixed support for a column."],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-045 (PARTLY) and DOUBTS; class 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1039,"term":"Braced versus unbraced frame: why the unbraced (moment) frame needs bigger members","topic":"Frames and bracing","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["why does an unbraced frame need bigger members","why does a moment frame need bigger members","braced frame connections are truss connections","unbraced frame connections are more complicated","unbraced frame is flexible and will move","braced frame versus unbraced frame trade offs","moment frame bigger members flexible","braced frame is truss like"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"We have an unbraced frame that gives us a lot more flexibility. However, we have to use bigger members because it's flexible. It'll move.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"Much more complicated. All of these connections are truss connections. All equal to zeroto zero.","date":"2026-09-09"}],"summary":"A braced frame is truss-like: its connections are truss connections (moment zero). An unbraced (moment) frame gives a lot more flexibility for the layout, but it is flexible and will move, so its members have to be bigger, and its connections are much more complicated (they have to carry moment). Her slide adds: flexibility for architectural design and layout, but greater deflection and drift than braced frames or shear walls.","answers":["Why does an unbraced (moment) frame need bigger members than a braced frame?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-051 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1040,"term":"A round pipe or round HSS has only one radius of gyration","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["why does a round column have only one r","pipe column radius of gyration","round hss one r","only one radius of gyration","because it's round there's only one r","circular section r","10 inch standard pipe column r","how many r values for a pipe"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Because it's round there's only 1 and I look up R and it's 3. 68 and it's structural steel.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"If it's round, there's only one r and look up the A.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"A round section (pipe or round HSS) has only one radius of gyration to look up, 'because it's round there's only 1', so the slenderness ratio uses that single r instead of separate x and y values. She said it for the 10-inch standard pipe column (9/09) and again for the round HSS sample problem (9/30).","answers":["Why does a round pipe column have only one r to look up?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-057 (PARTLY); classes 2026-09-09 and 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1041,"term":"Why the elastic (Euler) buckling equation has no material strength in it","topic":"Compression members","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why does the euler equation have no material strength","why is there no fy in the elastic buckling equation","buckling happens before the material strength is exhausted","how good the material is does not matter in buckling","elastic equation material strength","unstable before the material strength is exhausted","why no fy in euler","why does euler not include the steel strength"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"It kind of makes sense because when a compression member buckles, we don't know how good it's material is because it becomes unstable before we exhaust the material strength.","date":"2026-09-09"}],"summary":"A member that buckles becomes unstable before its material strength is exhausted, so you do not know (and it does not matter) how good its material is; that is why the elastic buckling equation contains no material strength. The other equation, the empirical one for the crushing range, does contain the material strength.","answers":["Why does the pin-ended elastic buckling equation contain no material strength?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-066 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1042,"term":"Table 4-14 or the formula: use the formula if Fy is not in the table","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["table or formula for column stress","which is preferred table 4-14 or the equations","what if fy is not in the table","steel strength not in the column table","go to the formula","is the table better than the formula","what do you need to know to use the stress table","the table is set up from 1 to 200"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Is one way more preferable than the other? No. All you need to know is, \"What is your steel strength?\". If you have something other than this then go to the formula.","date":"2026-09-09"}],"summary":"Neither the table nor the formula is preferred: all you need to know is your steel strength. If the steel strength is one of the table's columns (named in class: 35, 36, 46, 50 and 70 ksi; her slide also lists 65) read the table; if you have something other than this, go to the formula. The table is set up for KL/r from 1 to 200.","answers":["The column steel has a yield stress that is not one of the Table 4-14 columns; what do you do?","Is the table or the formula preferred for the critical stress?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-079 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1043,"term":"Why I-shapes are used as columns if a box is stronger (connections)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why use i shapes if a box is stronger","why not box columns","why are wide flanges used for columns","box versus wide flange column","connections to the beam wide flange column","reinforce a column with a box","why is the box not used as a column","box more rigid than a reinforced i"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"That's a good question. It's the connections to the beam as a column. But, if we have a section as a column and you want to reinforce it, as our colleague said, it would be much different as a box.","date":"2026-09-09"}],"summary":"A student asked why wide-flange (I) shapes are used if a box is so much stronger. Her answer: it is the connections to the beam; but when an existing section is used as a column and you want to reinforce it, a box (plates beside the web) is much better. She was answering a student's question, not setting an exam question.","answers":["A box is much stiffer than plates on the flanges; why are I-shapes used anyway?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-093 (NO); class 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1044,"term":"A real building cited for a box-reinforced column (garbled captions)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["real building where columns were reinforced into a box","building where the level buckled","columns reinforced with plates to make a box","the level that buckled","construction load on the plates","pfizer building columns","failure of a reinforced column example","plates welded to columns real building"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Just referencing the Pfizer building, what they showed us though columns was supposed to be reinforced to make a box. And the level that buckled, they didn't want to use the F word failed.","date":"2026-09-09"}],"summary":"While discussing welded plates on the W10x39 column she cited a real building (the captions say the Pfizer building): its columns were supposed to be reinforced with plates to make a box, and 'the level that buckled' (she avoided the word failed). The captions after that ('What happened to the plates? They brought the construction led load.') are garbled, so the rest of the story is uncertain.","answers":["In the real-building example she cited, what were the columns supposed to be reinforced into?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-094 (NO) and DOUBTS (uncertain); class 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1045,"term":"Weak axis braced, strong axis not: the strong axis uses the full length","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["which unbraced length goes with strong axis buckling","put your thumb on the axis you can understand","strong axis uses the full length","braces only on the weak axis","how to tell which length goes with which axis","thumb trick for column axes","weak axis braced strong axis not braced","why kx lx is the full length"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"What my teacher used to say is: Put your thumb on the axis you can understand if it's oriented this way, it's buckling the strong axis is the full length.","date":"2026-09-16"}],"summary":"When the weak axis is braced at points and the strong axis is not, strong-axis buckling uses the full length of the column (for example K = 0.8 on the full 28 ft in homework 5-17), while the braces shorten only the weak-axis length. Her own teacher's trick was to 'put your thumb on the axis you can understand' and see which way the column buckles.","answers":["The weak axis is braced at points and the strong axis is not; which unbraced length goes with strong-axis buckling?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-106 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-16","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1046,"term":"Which column to pick when several work (W14x53, W12x50, W10x49)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why choose the w10x49","w14x53 w12x50 w10x49 all carry 492 kips","which of several columns that work do you pick","different profiles fit the detail better","cheaper but fits better in the detail","play with it until something works","why not always the lightest column","lightest versus fits the detail"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"A 10x49 is a little bit cheaper, but it fits better in the detail. You can play a lot with it until you find something that works for you.","date":"2026-09-16"}],"summary":"The W14x53, W12x50 and W10x49 all carry the 492 kip load at KL = 10 ft; they have different profiles. The W10x49 is a little bit cheaper but fits better in the detail, and she said you can play a lot with it until you find something that works for you. (The reader notes that the reasoning in the captions is unclear.)","answers":["W14x53, W12x50 and W10x49 all carry 492 k at KL 10 ft; why might you choose the W10x49?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-125 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-16","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1047,"term":"Truss compression members are designed like columns","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["how is a compression member of a truss designed","what sets the unbraced length of a truss member","truss unbraced length panel length","truss members designed like columns","unbraced length from the framing","truss compression chord column tables","tension members of a truss designed as tension members","spanning members very deep and long"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"We're going to note the unbraced length that has to do with the framing, one direction is the panel length and other direction is where do the gains come in and use these tables?","date":"2026-09-16"}],"summary":"A truss is a very deep and long spanning member; like all spanning members its top chord is in compression and its bottom chord in tension. Its compression members are designed exactly the way columns are, with the same tables, whether vertical or horizontal; the unbraced lengths come from the framing (one direction is the panel length, the other is where the bracing comes in), and its tension members are designed as in tension design. The design project asks you to design a truss.","answers":["How is a compression member of a truss designed, and what sets its unbraced lengths?","What will the design project ask you to design?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md items B-132, B-135 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-16","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1048,"term":"Truss top chord: method of joints, then the column tables","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["how do you find the compression force in a truss top chord","method of joints then column tables","truss chord pure compression member","design a truss in the design project","vertical horizontal or diagonal compression member tables","which tables for a compression only member","truss top chords are pure compression","do the method of joints"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Eventually, I'm going to hand out the dreaded design project and one of the elements you're going to have to design is a truss.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"Turns out the top chords are pure compression members and once you do the method of joints, and you find out what the compression is, you can look it up.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"In the design project one of the elements you design is a truss. Its top chords are pure compression members: do the method of joints, find out what the compression is, then look it up in the column tables. It does not matter whether the member is vertical, horizontal or diagonal: if it only resists compression you can use the column tables (they presume the same unbraced length in both directions).","answers":["How do you find the compression force in a truss top chord before using the column tables?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-153 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1049,"term":"Which axis a beam bends about (load plane, strong x-x, weak y-y)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"fact","synonyms":["a beam's load is perpendicular to which axis","which axis does a beam bend about","load perpendicular to the weak axis","bending about the strong axis x-x","ixx much greater than iyy for the same area","why use a wide flange for bending","beam bends about the weak axis","load in the plane of the weak axis y-y"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Where the load is perpendicular to the longitudinal axis. It could be perpendicular to the weak axis. Which means it bends in this direction.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"A beam's load is perpendicular to its longitudinal axis, and its response is bending. On her week-5 slide sketch the load acts in the plane of the weak axis y-y, so the section bends about its strong axis x-x, and she notes Ixx is much greater than Iyy for the same area (the slide poses the question why a wide flange is used; the answer she gave is the deeper-section entry). In class (9/23) she added that the load could be perpendicular to the weak axis, in which case the beam bends in that other direction. The economy table (Table 3-2) covers bending about the x axis only. The slide detail is the reader's reading of the page image.","answers":["A W beam is loaded in the plane of its web: about which axis does it bend, and how do Ixx and Iyy compare for the same area?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-3 (S5 p1) and B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-169 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-23 and slide week 5 p.1","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1050,"term":"Beam bending: top flange compression, bottom flange tension, force = M / depth","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"fact","synonyms":["which flange is in compression in a beam","positive bending top flange compression","concave upward bending","how do you find the flange force from the moment","moment divided by the depth gives the flange force","top flange in compression bottom flange in tension","compression and tension separated by the depth","flange force equals moment over depth"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Concave upward or positive the top is compression and the bottom is tension. Top flange in compression, bottom flange in tension separated by the distance","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"We take the moment and do we do by the depth of the Y flange.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"For a beam bent concave upward (positive bending) the top flange is in compression and the bottom flange in tension, separated by the depth of the beam. The force in each flange is found by taking the moment and dividing by the depth (her slide: bending is a couple, C = T and M = C d = T d). When the compression in the top flange exceeds its buckling capacity the flange buckles, like a column. (The caption of her division sentence is garbled.)","answers":["In a beam bent concave upward, which flange is in compression, and how is the flange force found?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-177 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1051,"term":"Encasing steel in concrete for fire: good news and bad news","topic":"Construction and fire","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["what are the good news and the bad news of fully encasing steel","fully encased steel is fireproof","extra weight of encasing steel","encase only the top flange","fireproof what sticks below the top flange","fireproofing by encasement","encased beam weight","why not encase every steel beam in concrete"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"If you have steel fully encased, the good news is the steel is fireproof. The bad news is you're carrying a lot of extra weight.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"Maybe the only thing that needs to be incased is the top flange which was true, but it does mean we have to fireproof what sticks below it.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"If the steel is fully encased, the good news is that it is fireproof; the bad news is that you carry a lot of extra weight. Over time it became more sophisticated: perhaps only the top flange needs to be encased, but then you still have to fireproof whatever sticks out below it, which is still a lot of extra weight. An encased beam, an encased top flange and a composite slab are cases where the compression flange counts as continuously braced.","answers":["What are the good news and the bad news of fully encasing steel in concrete?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-182 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1052,"term":"Shear studs about 6 in on center brace the compression flange","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"reason","synonyms":["how far apart are the shear studs","why do shear studs brace the compression flange","studs welded 6 inches on center","the flange has to break the weld at every stud","how do we build floors today steel deck studs slab","why is a composite beam continuously braced","stud spacing on a composite beam","break that weld at every stud"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"How we build today is we lay down steel depth. We weld sheer studs and we power a concrete slab.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"In order for the compression flange to buckle it has to break that weld at every stud and they March along 6 inches on center.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"Today's floor: lay down the steel deck, weld shear studs to the beam and pour a concrete slab. For the compression flange to buckle sideways it would have to break the weld at every stud, and the studs march along about 6 in on center, so that is not what happens first and the compression flange is continuously braced (and the steel is still fireproofed).","answers":["How far apart are the shear studs on a composite beam, and why does that brace the flange?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-183 (PARTLY) and C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md M 9/23 #3a; class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1053,"term":"Details that give a continuously braced compression flange (and the upset beam)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"list","synonyms":["which details give continuous lateral bracing of the compression flange","name two details with a continuously braced compression flange","encased beam encased top flange composite construction","what is a fully unbraced beam example","upset beam deck below the top flange","anything other than a continuous brace is judgment","look closely at the details as designers","when is the compression flange continuously braced"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Anything other than this has to be your judgment and you have to be very careful as designers actually look at the details.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"We would have to weld it and maybe build a wall above it. This was not continuously lateral when braced. This was fully unbraced.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"Continuously braced compression flange (maximum moment capacity): a fully encased beam, an encased top flange, and composite construction (slab on a steel deck with welded studs). Anything other than these is your judgment, and as a designer you have to look very closely at the details: for example, if the beam is set so that the metal deck sits below the top flange (an 'upset' beam) you would have to weld it and maybe build a wall above it, and that is not continuously braced; it is treated as fully unbraced.","answers":["Name two construction details in which the compression flange can be taken as continuously laterally braced, and one that needs judgment."],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-1 (S5 p5, M 9/23 #3b) and B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-185; class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1054,"term":"A steel beam in a wood building: the designer decides (her story)","topic":"Construction and fire","kind":"fact","synonyms":["steel beam in a wood building","fire department and the buildings department objected","who decides when the detail is not the standard one","the designer has to make a decision","dob and fire department steel beam wood building","designer judgment fire protection"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"There was a time when the fire department if I have a steel beam and a wood building there was a time when the DOB and the fire department says it's not placed.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"You as the designer has to make a decision.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"She told a story about a time when the buildings department and the fire department objected to a steel beam in a wood building, and ended 'You as the designer has to make a decision': when the standard detail does not settle it, the designer decides. The captions of the story are garbled, so the answer is uncertain beyond that.","answers":["Who decides what to do when the bracing or fire-protection detail is not the standard one?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-186 (NO) and DOUBTS; class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1055,"term":"We never design in ASD (use the LRFD columns of the economy table)","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"fact","synonyms":["when might you need asd capacities","we never design in asd","renovating a pre lrfd building","which columns of the economy table do you use for lrfd","white columns with blue lettering","omega b 1.67 asd phi b 0.90 lrfd","economy table asd or lrfd","asd only for old buildings"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"We never design in AS B. If you have renovated a building built preL RFD, you might have to know what capacity are.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"To do this, we only design that when we're looking at the white columns with the blue lettering.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"She said 'We never design in ASD'; you might need to know ASD capacities only if you renovate a building built before LRFD. In the economy table she pointed to the LRFD columns ('the white columns with the blue lettering', as heard; which columns these are cannot be checked from the captions). The foot of the table page on her slide prints phi_b = 0.90 for LRFD and Omega_b = 1.67 for ASD (the reader's reading of the page image).","answers":["When might you need ASD capacities, and which columns of the economy table do you use for LRFD?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-189 (PARTLY) and C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-12 (S5 p6); class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1056,"term":"Why steel and wood framing can be counted as a psf number but concrete cannot","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why can steel framing be counted as a psf load","why is concrete not a psf number","an unusually large member is weighed separately","steel and wood are lightweight","concrete is very heavy and holds itself up","why does concrete need its own weight calculated","framing weight psf steel and wood","most of the work a concrete structure does is hold itself up"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"for the kind of loads that these two materials carry, they are pretty lightweight and treat it in a pound per square foot number. That won't be so next semester in concrete because concrete is very heavy.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"So we have most of the work that a concrete structure does is hold itself up.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"Steel and wood framing are taken as a pound-per-square-foot number because the loads these two materials carry are pretty lightweight; an unusually large member is looked at separately (caption garble). That will not be so next semester in concrete, because concrete is very heavy and most of the work a concrete structure does is hold itself up.","answers":["Why can steel framing be counted as a psf load but concrete cannot?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-208 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1057,"term":"First failure sets the capacity: the compression flange of a wide flange","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["what fails first in bending of a wide flange","once the compression flange buckles we are done","what decides the capacity of a concrete beam","first section to fail is the capacity","what cracks first in concrete","compression flange fails first","end of the road compression flange","first failure beams columns tension"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Whenever we are looking at a phenomenon failure load, maximum load and the thing that fails first for bending in a wide flange is the compression flange. Once that buckles, we are done.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"It'll be the reverse when we do concrete. So we'll be looking at what cracks first. As soon as it cracks that's the end of the line.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"For bending of a wide flange the thing that fails first is the compression flange: once it buckles you are done, as soon as the first part fails that is the capacity (the same first-failure idea as for tension parts and the column KL/r values). It will be the reverse when you do concrete (next semester): you look at what cracks first, and as soon as it cracks that is the end of the line. The tension flange is fine as long as it is not punched full of holes.","answers":["What decides the capacity of a concrete beam, unlike a steel wide flange?","In bending of a wide flange, which part fails first?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md items B-215, B-216, B-217 (NO); class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1058,"term":"The load is the load: the moment does not depend on the material","topic":"Analysis and design","kind":"fact","synonyms":["does the moment depend on the material","the load is the load","a 62 kip load at mid span of a girder gives 310 kip-ft whatever the material","same moment for a concrete beam","material decides how you design for the moment","moment is the same for steel and concrete","does the load change with the material"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"The load is the load. Concrete beam and it had a 62 kip force at mid span. The moment will still be 310 kips.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"How you design and what's happen to it. That's the material base. The load is the load.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"The load, and the moment it causes, do not depend on the material: a 62 kip load at mid-span of the 20 ft girder gives 310 kip-ft whether the beam is steel or concrete. What differs is how you design for it, which is the material's part ('That's the material base'). (The caption writes the moment as 310 kips; it is kip-ft.)","answers":["A 62 k point load acts at mid-span of a 20 ft girder; does the moment depend on the material?"],"source":"B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-218 (PARTLY); class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1059,"term":"Concrete versus steel: tension side, hanger versus column","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["why is the steel on the tension side of a concrete beam","why use steel in a concrete beam","concrete good in compression brittle in tension","hanger versus column steel needed","a hanger needs less steel than a column","two materials in the best way possible","concrete is the opposite of steel","which material is good in pulling","steel in a hanger pulls","steel columns squash or bow"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"With concrete is the opposite. It's really good in compression. It's very brittle in tension. That's why the steel is on the tension side and the concrete is on the compression side.","date":"2026-09-30"},{"quote":"We're using two materials in the best way possible. Load is the same. A lot less steel as a hanger than as a because steel has really good intention.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"Steel is very good in tension if you do not make Swiss cheese of it; the weak link is compression, because of buckling, so a compression member needs more steel. Concrete is the opposite: very good in compression and very brittle in tension, which is why the steel goes on the tension side and the concrete on the compression side of a concrete beam (two materials used in the best way possible). The load is the same, but a tension hanger needs a lot less steel than a column. (Caption garbles: 'intention' = tension.)","answers":["Why does a reinforced-concrete beam carry its steel on the tension side?","Which needs less steel for the same load, a hanger or a column?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-2 (9/30 #77b); class 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1060,"term":"Yield moment My: the extreme fiber first reaches Fy (her week-5 slide)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["what is the yield moment","first yield moment my","extreme fiber reaches fy","stage 1 of bending","my equals fy s","when does a beam start to yield","outer fibre yields first","bending stress at the extreme fiber","statics stopped when the extreme fiber yielded"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"The magnitude of the bending stress at the extreme fiber reaches the yield point Fy of the material","date":null},{"quote":"And we allowed the stress level to increase until the extreme fiber started to yield and when that happened? We stopped. That's as far as we went in statics.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"Stage 1 of bending on her week-5 slide: the bending stress at the extreme fiber reaches the yield point Fy of the material. In the elastic range the extreme-fiber stress is fb = M/S (marked 'statics' on the slide), so at first yield Fy = My/S and My = Fy S (the formulas are the reader's reading of the slide image). In statics she let the stress rise only until the extreme fiber started to yield and then stopped.","answers":["At the instant the extreme fiber first reaches Fy, what is the moment called and how is it computed?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-4 (S5 p2); slide week 5 p.2 and class 2026-09-23","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1061,"term":"Beyond My the yielding spreads inward (stage 2 of bending)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"reason","synonyms":["what happens when the moment is greater than the yield moment","stress is transferred to fibers that are not yet at yield","yielding spreads through the depth","the extreme fiber cannot exceed fy","stage 2 of bending","plastic zone of a beam","m greater than my","yielding moves inward from the outer fibre"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"M > My Stress at the extreme fiber cannot exceed Fy","date":null},{"quote":"Stress is transferred fibers that are not yet @ yield point","date":null},{"quote":"It starts the permeate the entire depth until the entire depth of the section has yielded it.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"Stage 2 on her week-5 slide: when M is greater than My, the stress at the extreme fiber cannot exceed Fy, so stress is transferred to fibers that are not yet at the yield point. In class (9/23) she said the yielding then goes through the entire depth of the section until the whole section has yielded; once the extreme fiber yields you do not get more stress there.","answers":["What happens in a beam when the moment goes beyond the yield moment My?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-4 (S5 p2); slide week 5 p.2 and class 2026-09-23","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1062,"term":"Full plasticity M = Mp: maximum bending strength (stage 3)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["what is full plasticity","stage 3 of bending","why is strain hardening not used in beams","maximum bending strength of the section","mp equals fy z","the end of the rope for us","beyond mp the strains are too large","why do we stop at the plastic moment","full plastic moment"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"M = Mp Full plasticity of the cross section = maximum bending strength of the section","date":null},{"quote":"beyond this point the extra stress capacity due strain hardening is impractical because the strains are too large","date":null}],"summary":"Stage 3 on her week-5 slide: M = Mp, full plasticity of the cross section, is the maximum bending strength of the section; beyond that point the extra stress capacity from strain hardening is impractical because the strains are too large. Mp = Fy Z (for the rectangle she derived, Z = b d^2/4, with S = b d^2/6 and a shape factor Mp/My = 1.5; these formulas are the reader's reading of the slide image). On 9/23 she did the rectangle once and said she would not do it again.","answers":["What does full plasticity mean for a beam, and why is strain-hardening strength not counted?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-4 (S5 p2-p3); slide week 5 p.2 and class 2026-09-23","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1063,"term":"Beam, and the members that act as beams (her week-5 slide)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"list","synonyms":["what is a beam","which members act as beams","joists lintels spandrels stringers girders","retaining walls are beams","define a beam","list members that are beams","a structural element that rests on supports","horizontal member that resists loads perpendicular to its axis"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Beam: A structural element (usually horizontal), that rests on supports & resists loads perpendicular to its longitudinal axis","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-5 slide: a beam is a structural element, usually horizontal, that rests on supports and resists loads perpendicular to its longitudinal axis. Members that are beams: joists, lintels, spandrels, stringers, girders, retaining walls, and others (the list is open-ended, 'etc').","answers":["Name four kinds of member that act as beams."],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-7 (S5 p1); slide week 5 p.1","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1064,"term":"Beam diagrams: Table 3-23, enough about 85 percent of the time","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"fact","synonyms":["how often are the beam diagrams enough","where are the beam diagrams","table 3-23 shears moments and deflections","common beam loadings at the back of the beam chapter","statics or the manual for the maximum moment","85 percent of the time","beam diagrams pages 3-208 to 3-221","diagram 1 diagram 7 diagram 9"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Refer to AISC Table 3-23 pg 3-208 to 3-221 Shears, Moments & Deflections","date":null},{"quote":"I would say 85 percent of the time, you can just use these diagrams in the beam section towards the back.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"For the maximum moment you can use statics or go to the Steel Manual: the beam chapter has common beam loadings (diagrams) at the back, and she said that about 85 percent of the time you can just use these diagrams. Her week-5 slide cites AISC Table 3-23, pages 3-208 to 3-221, for shears, moments and deflections. The diagrams she used: diagram 1 (uniform load, wL^2/8), diagram 7 (single load at the center, PL/4), diagram 9 (two equal loads, Pa).","answers":["How often can the common beam diagrams in the Manual replace a statics calculation?","Which Manual table does her slide cite for shears, moments and deflections of beams?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-5, NC-6 (9/30 #10, S5 p7); slide week 5 p.7 and class 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1065,"term":"Total equivalent uniform load row of the beam diagrams","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what is the total equivalent uniform load","equivalent uniform load for a point load at midspan","2p for diagram 7","8pa over l for diagram 9","4wl for diagram 19","8p for diagram 22","equivalent uniform load row of the beam diagrams"],"not":[],"definitions":[],"summary":"SUMMARY (not her words; no clear caption or slide line to quote): On her week-5 slides each beam diagram also prints a total equivalent uniform load: wl for diagram 1, 2P for diagram 7 (a single load at the center of a simple span), 8Pa/l for diagram 9, 4wl for the cantilever with a uniform load (diagram 19) and 8P for the cantilever with a point load at the free end (diagram 22). It was shown but never discussed in class (the reader read it from the page images).","answers":["What total equivalent uniform load corresponds to a single load P at the center of a simple span?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-11 (S5 p7, p8); slides week 5 p.7-8","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1066,"term":"Economy table is for Fy 50 and the x axis only; Table 3-4 is the y axis","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"fact","synonyms":["which steel strength is the economy table for","economy table fy 50 only","economy table x axis only","table 3-4 bending about the y axis","what is table 3-4","grouped by similar zx","economy table limits","can i use the economy table for another steel strength"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Table is set up for Fy=50 ksi steel only","date":null},{"quote":"Bending about the X axis only (AISC Table 3-4: Bending about the Y axis)","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-5 slide on the economy tables (Table 3-2, pages 3-19 to 3-27): the table is set up for Fy = 50 ksi steel only; it covers bending about the x axis only (AISC Table 3-4 is for bending about the y axis); shapes are grouped by similar Zx and similar phi Mpx (moment capacity), and within a grouping the most economical is in boldface type.","answers":["For which steel strength and which axis of bending is the economy table set up, and which table covers the y axis?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-5 (S5 p6); slide week 5 p.6","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1067,"term":"What braces the compression flange of a girder: the beams that frame into it","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what braces a girder","girder braced by the beams framing into it","unbraced length of a girder","beams at the third points of the girder","what provides lateral bracing in a floor system","braced only by the beams that frame into it","what is the unbraced length of the compression flange of a girder","lateral support of a girder"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"It's braced only by the beams that frame into it.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"In a floor system the compression flange of a girder is braced by the beams that frame into it, so its unbraced length is their spacing (her week-5 slide sketch shows a girder with beams framing in at its third points; her 9/30 example with an 8 ft unbraced length: 'braced only by the beams that frame into it'). The slide sketch detail is the reader's reading of the page image.","answers":["In a floor system what braces the compression flange of a girder, and at what spacing?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-8 (S5 p4; 9/30 #53); slide week 5 p.4 and class 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1068,"term":"Span length gives the moment load; the unbraced length gives the capacity","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"reason","synonyms":["what does the span fix and what does the unbraced length fix","span length gives us moment load","unbraced length gives us capacity","does the unbraced length change the moment","the moment doesn't change the unbraced length changes","a longer unbraced length means less capacity","span versus unbraced length","does lb enter mu"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Span length gives us moment load, the unbraced length gives us capacity. Big difference.","date":"2026-09-30"},{"quote":"The moment doesn't change. The unbraced length changes.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"For a beam, the span length gives the moment load (the moment to be resisted); the unbraced length of the compression flange gives the capacity. When the unbraced length gets longer, like a column that gets more spaghetti-like, the compression the flange can carry goes down and so the moment capacity goes down. The moment does not change; the unbraced length changes the capacity. (The unbraced-length curves themselves are taught after the midterm cut.)","answers":["What does the span fix and what does the unbraced length fix for a beam?","Does the unbraced length of the compression flange change the moment Mu?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-10 (9/30 #48, #61); class 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1069,"term":"Why a deeper section carries more moment (why wide flanges)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why does a deeper section carry more moment","why do we use wide flanges for beams","deeper member more moment same steel","flanges farther apart more moment","most of the moment of inertia is flange area times distance squared","why is a w shape organized the way it is","same area deeper section","ad squared deeper beam"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"The deeper the member is the further away these two flanges are the more moment it can resist.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"Without adding additional seal we make it deeper. We are increasing the moment capacity.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"So for the same, A and make it deeper you get a lot more capacity.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"Bending resolves into a couple (one flange acts like a compression member and the other like a tension member), so the deeper the member, the farther apart the two flanges and the more moment it can resist, without adding steel. For the same area, making it deeper gives a lot more capacity, because most of the moment of inertia is the area of the flange times its distance squared (A d^2). (Caption garble: 'seal' = steel.)","answers":["Why does a deeper section carry much more moment than a shallower one of the same area?","Why are wide-flange shapes used for beams?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md NC-3, NC-15 (M 9/23 #2); B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-170, B-200; class 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1070,"term":"Euler check: compute the slenderness ratio first","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["when is the euler equation reasonable","do the slenderness ratio first","euler columns have to be very slender","round hss 7 x 0.25 sample problem","oiler column","when do i use the critical stress equation directly","how slender must a column be for euler","check slenderness before using euler"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Because that will tell me if it's reasonable to be using oiler. They have to be very slender to be oiler columns.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"In her round-HSS sample (HSS 7 x 0.25, K = 1, L = 32 ft, only one r) she did the slenderness ratio first (KL/r about 161), because it tells you whether it is reasonable to use Euler: Euler columns have to be very slender. (Caption garble: 'oiler' = Euler.) Then the Euler stress (about 11 ksi) times the area gives the critical buckling load, which she compared with the Manual's HSS column table.","answers":["Why do the slenderness ratio first in the Euler sample problem?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md G10 (9/30 #85, #86); class 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1071,"term":"Column table page: check the Fy at the top (ordinary steel is Fy 50)","topic":"The Manual","kind":"fact","synonyms":["which fy do i use in the column table","make sure you're at the correct fy","fy is at the top of the page","we're not using exotic steel","ordinary steel fy 50 column table","check the steel strength on the table page","what steel strength do i assume","which page of the column tables"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Make sure you're at the correct Fy. We're not using exotic steel. It'll be Fy50. It's at the top of the page.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"When reading the column tables, make sure you are at the correct Fy: it is printed at the top of the page. In her 9/30 sample she said they are not using exotic steel, so it is Fy 50, then look at the W8 rows and the KL row.","answers":["How do you know which Fy a column-table page is for?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md G11 (9/30 #76); class 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1072,"term":"Why fixed ends carry more load (like a shorter column)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why does a fixed end column carry more load","why is k 0.65 for fixed fixed","resistance to rotation is the same as a shorter column","shorter distance between points of zero moment","why is the effective length shorter when the ends are fixed","fixed fixed k smaller than pinned pinned","end restraint and capacity","why fixed ends help a column"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Resistance to rotation is the same as the shorter column. A shorter distance of points of 0 moment. If the length is shorter, we can carry more load.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"Fixed ends resist rotation, which is the same as a shorter column: a shorter distance between the points of zero moment. If the length is shorter, we can carry more load (K = 0.65 for fixed-fixed against 1.0 for pinned-pinned).","answers":["Why does a column with fixed ends carry more load than a pinned one?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md G12 (9/30 #75); class 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1073,"term":"Adding braces to the compression flange raises the moment (her slide)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why does bracing the compression flange increase the moment capacity","more braces shorter unbraced length more moment","brace at mid span versus third points","adding braces to the compression flange","what is the maximum moment capacity of a beam","continuously laterally braced gives maximum moment","the flange bows sideways less when braced","bracing the top flange"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Adding braces to the compression flange -> decreasing the unbraced length increasing the axial load C required to buckle the top flange increasing the amount of M that can be resisted & the applied load","date":null},{"quote":"Therefore: Maximum Moment capacity -> the compression flange is continuously laterally braced","date":null},{"quote":"Same game; same n flange, but this time I'm going to brace it at the third points. So there was going to be a moment greater than the second moment. A lot greater than the first moment.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"Her week-5 slide: adding braces to the compression flange decreases the unbraced length, which increases the axial load C required to buckle the top flange, which increases the moment that can be resisted and the load that can be applied. In class: brace it once and the moment it can carry is greater than the first; brace it at the third points and it is greater than the second and a lot greater than the first. Therefore the maximum moment capacity is reached when the compression flange is continuously laterally braced.","answers":["Why does bracing the compression flange of a beam raise the moment it can carry?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md (S5 p4, p5); B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md items B-178, B-179; slide week 5 p.4-5 and class 2026-09-23","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1074,"term":"A flat ceiling: choose the girder as deep as the beams (W16x50)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"fact","synonyms":["when can the lightest member be rejected","flat ceiling girder same depth as the beams","why choose the w16x50 girder","depth requirement overrides lightest","bottom of the flanges at the same elevation","most economical versus flat ceiling","is there a w16 that works","both are solutions"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Is there something that works so that the bottom of the flanges are at the same elevation? Is there a W16 that works?","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"Both are solutions the W21 is the most economical. They both work.","date":"2026-09-23"},{"quote":"You want the ceiling to be flat.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"For the 20 ft girder (Mu = 310 kip-ft, fully braced) the most economical is the W21x44; but if you want the bottom of the flanges at the same elevation as the beams (a flat ceiling) you ask whether a W16 works: the W16x50 does (345 kip-ft). Both are solutions; the W21 is the most economical. A depth requirement is a very good reason to pick something that is not the lightest.","answers":["The lightest girder is deeper than the beams; what do you choose if the ceiling must be flat?","When can the lightest section be rejected?"],"source":"C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md inventory #60 and B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-220; classes 2026-09-23 and 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1075,"term":"Table 2-4 legend: black square, shaded cell, blank cell","topic":"The Manual","kind":"definition","synonyms":["what does the black square mean in table 2-4","table 2-4 legend","preferred material specification","shaded cell in table 2-4","blank cell in table 2-4","other applicable material specification","confirm availability before specifying","which steel is preferred for a shape"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Preferred material specification; light-shaded cell = Other applicable material specification (availability should be confirmed prior to specification); blank/white cell = Material specification does not apply.","date":null}],"summary":"The legend printed under Table 2-4 (shown on her week-1 slide 4): a black square marks the preferred material specification for that shape; a light-shaded cell marks another applicable specification (confirm availability before specifying it); a blank cell means the specification does not apply to that shape.","answers":["In the Manual table of applicable ASTM specifications, what do a black square, a shaded cell and a blank cell mean?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 1 slide 4 (Table 2-4 legend, PARTLY); slide week 1 p.4","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1076,"term":"Carbon content: mild, medium and high carbon steel (percentages)","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"list","synonyms":["what carbon range defines medium carbon steel","mild steel carbon percentage","high carbon steel percentage","carbon content of structural steel in 1890","how much carbon is in mild steel","carbon classes of steel","low medium and high carbon steel","percent carbon in steel","what are the carbon percentages"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"1890 Structural Steel replaced Wrought Iron in construction","date":null},{"quote":"Carbon Content: 0.15% to 1.7%","date":null},{"quote":"Mild steel 0.15% - 0.29%","date":null},{"quote":"Medium carbon steel 0.30% - 0.59%","date":null},{"quote":"High carbon steel 0.60% - 1.70%","date":null},{"quote":"And today, we even have mild, medium and high carbon steel.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"On her week-1 slide p.5: structural steel replaced wrought iron in construction in 1890, with a carbon content of 0.15% to 1.7%; mild steel 0.15% - 0.29%; medium carbon steel 0.30% - 0.59%; high carbon steel 0.60% - 1.70%. In class (8/26) she said that today there are mild, medium and high carbon steels.","answers":["What carbon range defines medium carbon steel?","What is the carbon content of mild steel?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 1 slide 5 (carbon classes, PARTLY); slide week 1 p.5 and class 2026-08-26","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1077,"term":"Cast iron and wrought iron: carbon content on her slide","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["which iron is high carbon","cast iron carbon content","wrought iron carbon content","cast iron has low carbon","wrought iron has high carbon","difference between cast iron and wrought iron","cast iron brittle wrought iron malleable","1840 wrought iron replaced cast iron","which has more carbon cast or wrought iron"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Cast Iron: low carbon content (<= 0.15%)","date":null},{"quote":"Wrought Iron: high carbon content (>= 2.00%)","date":null},{"quote":"1840 Wrought Iron began to replace Cast Iron","date":null},{"quote":"Less brittle, again, high carbon content, but could span longer distances.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Her version, as written on her week-1 slide p.5: 1779, the first cast-iron bridge (England), revolutionary but brittle, with cast iron low in carbon (<= 0.15%); 1840, wrought iron began to replace cast iron, more malleable, with wrought iron high in carbon (>= 2.00%). In class (8/26, the captions say 'water iron') she described wrought iron as less brittle, 'again, high carbon content', able to span longer distances. This entry stores her version only.","answers":["Which has more carbon, cast iron or wrought iron?","Which iron replaced the other in 1840, and which was more malleable?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 1 slide 5; A-classes-0826-0902.md inventory (8/26 history items); slide week 1 p.5 and class 2026-08-26","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1078,"term":"1856: steel first made in the USA, carbon added to iron (<= 1%)","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"fact","synonyms":["when was steel first made in the usa","1856 steel","how much carbon was added to iron in 1856","bessemer process carbon added to iron","when did structural steel replace wrought iron","carbon added to iron less than 1 percent","first steel in america","history of steel 1856 1890"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"1856 Steel first made in the USA","date":null},{"quote":"Carbon added to Iron (<= 1%) with the Bessemer Process (textbook pg. 6)","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-1 slide p.5: 1856, steel first made in the USA, with carbon added to the iron (<= 1%) by the steel-making process of the day (the Bessemer process, textbook p.6); 1890, structural steel replaced wrought iron in construction (carbon content 0.15% to 1.7%).","answers":["In 1856 steel was first made in the USA; how much carbon was added to the iron?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 1 slide 5 (1856 steel, PARTLY); slide week 1 p.5","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1079,"term":"Effect of carbon on steel: harder and stronger but more brittle","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what does carbon do to steel","effect of carbon on steel","more carbon more brittle","carbon gives hardness and strength","carbon and weldability","bad weldability","what do chromium silicon and nickel do","why not use more carbon","hard steel snaps"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Carbon gives hardness & strength, but the more carbon the more brittle (ex: bad weldability)","date":null},{"quote":"Chromium, silicon & nickel produce higher strength, but more expensive","date":null},{"quote":"carbon gives hardness and strength so you might think that's a good thing, but the more carbon you have the more brittle the steel is.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"Her week-1 slide p.4 and class 8/26: carbon gives hardness and strength, but the more carbon the more brittle the steel (for example, bad weldability); chromium, silicon and nickel give higher strength but are more expensive (project-specific decisions).","answers":["What does carbon do to the strength and the brittleness of steel?"],"source":"council O6 card 'Carbon content effects' (kept in her words); slide week 1 p.4 and class 2026-08-26","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1080,"term":"Why holes call for the rupture check at the net section (wk2 p.1)","topic":"Tension members","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why check rupture","why do bolt holes need a net section check","fail at the net section","reduced area at the holes","tear at the holes","fracture at the net section","why two checks for tension","strain hardening in the reduced area is quickly reached","why is the net area check needed"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"w/ Bolt holes - can fail @ the net section (reduced area @ the holes).","date":null},{"quote":"Ar @ load lower than the load required to yield the gross area.","date":null},{"quote":"Strain hardening @ the reduced area is quickly reached","date":null}],"summary":"On her week-2 slide 1, a member without holes can resist more than Ag Fy because of strain hardening, but it would lengthen a great deal; a member with bolt holes can fail at the net section (the reduced area at the holes) at a load lower than the load needed to yield the gross area, because strain hardening in the reduced area is quickly reached. Hence the code requires two checks: yielding at the gross area, or rupture at the effective net area. (The middle annotation is rough: 'Ar @ load lower than ...'.)","answers":["Why does a member with bolt holes need a rupture check?","Why does tension have two checks?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 2 slide 1 (why a net-section check, PARTLY); slide week 2 p.1","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1081,"term":"Block shear: what it is (her slide wk2 p.19-21)","topic":"Tension members","kind":"definition","synonyms":["what is block shear","block shear definition","tear out of a block of material at the bolts","shear plus tension failure","anv ant agv","block shear for rolled shapes","fy always with ag fu always with an","block shear u 1.0 single row 0.5 multiple rows","block shear phi 0.75"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"FOR ROLLED SHAPES ONLY - CHECK 'BLOCK SHEAR'","date":null},{"quote":"Note: 2 phenomena are going on simultaneously - Shear + Tension.","date":null},{"quote":"Anv = Net Area in Shear","date":null},{"quote":"Agv = Gross Area in Shear","date":null},{"quote":"note Fy always with Ag","date":null},{"quote":"Fu always with An","date":null}],"summary":"Her slides: for rolled shapes only, check block shear. Two phenomena go on at the same time, shear and tension: the block along the bolt rows tears out along shear planes (parallel to the load) and tension planes (perpendicular to the load). Anv = net area in shear, Ant = net area in tension, Agv = gross area in shear; phi = 0.75; U = 1.0 for a single-row connection and 0.5 for multiple rows; the strength is phi(0.6 Fu Anv + U Fu Ant) or phi(0.6 Fy Agv + U Fu Ant) (take the lower), with Fy always with the gross area and Fu always with the net area. Every homework says to neglect block shear and it was not on her 9/30 list of midterm topics.","answers":["What is block shear?","Which areas go with Fy and which with Fu in the block shear equation?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md section 'put outside the midterm' item 1 (block shear); slide week 2 p.19-21","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1082,"term":"Block shear example: the W21x93 flange (her slide wk2 p.22)","topic":"Tension members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["block shear example","w21x93 block shear","block shear strength of the w21x93 flange","does block shear govern for the w21x93","1044 k and 1030 k","agv anv ant of the w21x93","prior example block shear","996.5 k tmax"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Prior Example w/ W21x93 - Calculate Block Shear Strength","date":null},{"quote":"Note: Tmax still 996.5 k","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-2 slide 22 re-uses the W21x93 flange example (bolts 3/4 in, three holes in a line spaced 2, 4, 4 in, edge distance 1.5 in, four block-shear areas): Agv = 4(10)(0.93) = 37.2 in^2; Anv = 4[10 - 2.5(3/4 + 1/8)](0.93) = 29.1 in^2; Ant = 4[1.5 - (1/2)(3/4 + 1/8)](0.93) = 3.95 in^2. phi Pn = 0.75(0.6(65)(29.1) + 1(65)(3.95)) = 1044 k and 0.75(0.6(50)(37.2) + 1(65)(3.95)) = 1030 k, both above the earlier 996.5 k, so block shear did not govern. Nothing else in the weeks 1-4 slides uses block shear.","answers":["On her W21x93 example, did block shear govern, and what are the two block shear strengths?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md section 'put outside the midterm' item 1 (worked example, slide 22); slide week 2 p.22","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1083,"term":"Equal-area shapes: W21x50, W10x49 and a 12 in pipe (her Figure 9.8)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["which shape is least slender for the same area","w21x50 versus w10x49 versus 12 in standard pipe","round pipe is the least slender","same area different slenderness","figure 9.8","comparison of steel cross-sections with equivalent areas","why is a pipe a good column","which shape bows first for the same area","l over r for equal area shapes"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Figure 9.8 Comparison of steel cross-sections with equivalent areas.","date":null},{"quote":"The more slender direction is about the YY axis, r_y = r_min","date":null},{"quote":"L/r_y is the critical slenderness & will yield the first failure load","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-3 slide 4 (textbook Figure 9.8, three cross-sections of equal area, L = 15 ft): W21x50 (A = 14.7 in^2, ry about 1.3 in as its L/r implies) L/r = 138.5; W10x49 (A = 14.4, ry = 2.54) L/r = 70.9; 12 in standard pipe (A = 14.0, r = 4.38 about both axes) L/r = 41.1. So of the three the pipe is the least slender and the W21x50 the most. Where rx and ry differ, the more slender direction is about the y axis, r_y = r_min, and L/r_y is the critical slenderness and gives the first failure load. (The slide transcript prints ry = 1.90 for the W21x50, which does not match its L/r of 138.5; use the printed L/r values.)","answers":["Of a W21x50, a W10x49 and a 12 in standard pipe (about 14 in^2 each), which is least slender at 15 ft and what is its L/r?","A W10x49 has ry 2.54 in and L = 15 ft; what is L/r?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 3 slide 4 (equal-area shapes, PARTLY) and its list of slide text that disagrees with itself; slide week 3 p.4","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1084,"term":"Column splice details (2 ft above the floor, milled, in bearing)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["where is a column splice made","how high above the floor is the splice","milled contact surfaces","splice in bearing","keep t consistent at a splice","most of the load goes through in bearing","2 feet above finished floor","how is the upper column force passed on at a splice","why keep the flange the same between lifts"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Contact Surfaces milled","date":null},{"quote":"to transfer upper col. force in bearing","date":null},{"quote":"2' Above Fin. Floor","date":null},{"quote":"Most load will go through in bearing","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-4 slides 13-14 on the column splice: the splice is located 2 ft above the finished floor; the contact surfaces are milled so the upper column force is transferred in bearing; keep T consistent between the upper and lower lift sizes so that most of the load goes through in bearing. The splice plates carry the difference between the two lifts (in her example (724 - 441)/2 = 141.5 k per plate), each checked as a short compression member (KL = 3 in).","answers":["How high above the finished floor is the splice, and how is the upper column force passed on?","Why keep T consistent between the upper and lower lift sizes at a splice?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 4 slides 13-14 (splice details, PARTLY); slide week 4 p.13-14","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1085,"term":"Eqn 3-3: Fcr = 0.877 Fe (Euler with a safety factor of about 1.15)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what is equation 3-3","fcr equals 0.877 fe","where does the 0.877 come from","elastic buckling line","euler equation with a safety factor","safety factor 1.15 in the elastic buckling equation","which equation for the long column","the elastic buckling equation of the manual"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"EQN 3-3 (elastic buckling): F_cr = 0.877 F_e","date":null},{"quote":"NOTE: Euler Equation w/ SF ~= 1.15","date":null},{"quote":"One is the oiler equation except we added a safety factor to it and the other one is an empirical formula.","date":"2026-09-09"}],"summary":"Her week-3 slide 15 labels the two branches of the Fcr against KL/r diagram: Eqn 3-2 (inelastic buckling, an empirical equation based on the material strength, roughly parabolic) and Eqn 3-3 (elastic buckling), Fcr = 0.877 Fe, which she notes is the Euler equation with a safety factor of about 1.15. The two meet at KL/r = 4.71 sqrt(E/Fy), and the elastic branch has a limit of 200. In class (9/09) she said one is the Euler equation with a safety factor added and the other is an empirical formula.","answers":["Eqn 3-3, Fcr = 0.877 Fe, is the elastic formula with what safety factor?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 3 slide 15 (safety factor in Eqn 3-3, PARTLY); slide week 3 p.15 and class 2026-09-09","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1086,"term":"Mild steel stress-strain figure: upper and lower yield points (Figure 5.22)","topic":"Stress-strain curve","kind":"list","synonyms":["name the two yield points on the mild steel diagram","upper yield point and lower yield point","figure 5.22","ranges on the stress strain diagram of mild steel","elastic inelastic plastic strain hardening range","true rupture stress","what is the elastic range","points on the mild steel stress strain curve"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Figure 5.22 Stress-strain diagram for mild steel (A36) with key points highlighted.","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-3 slide 4 reuses textbook Figure 5.22, the stress-strain diagram for mild steel (A36). Points labeled: proportional limit, upper yield point, lower yield point, ultimate stress, true stress / true rupture, rupture; ranges labeled: elastic, inelastic, plastic and strain hardening, with the 'allowable' stress level marked. A red box on her slide marks the elastic range, the region where the Euler equation is valid. (The point and range names are the transcript's reading of the page image.)","answers":["Name the two yield points drawn on the mild-steel stress-strain diagram."],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 3 slide 4 (Figure 5.22 labels, PARTLY); slide week 3 p.4","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1087,"term":"Flange slopes: S and C 16 2/3 percent, W 0 to 5 percent","topic":"Shape names","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what flange slope do s beams and c channels have","w flange taper","slope of the inner flange of an s shape","16 2/3 percent slope","why is an m shape not a w s or hp","tapered flange of a wide flange","flange taper 0 to 5 percent","american standard beam slope"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"as the slope of their inner flanges is other than 16 2/3 percent.","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-1 slide 7 (textbook Figure 1.1): the flanges of the S beam (American standard beam) and the C channel have a 16 2/3 percent slope; the flange of a W section has a slope of 0 to 5 percent. An M shape is a doubly symmetrical H shape that cannot be classed as a W, S or HP section because the slope of its inner flanges is other than 16 2/3 percent. (The slope figures come from the slide transcript's reading of the figure labels.)","answers":["What flange slope do S beams and C channels have, and what is the W-flange taper?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 1 slide 7 (flange slopes, PARTLY); slide week 1 p.7","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1088,"term":"The New York building of the 1850s on her history slide","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what was new about the new york building of the 1850s","first building with wrought iron i-beams in new york","precursor of skeletal steel frame construction","six story foundation building","1853 1858 first buildings with wrought iron frames","wrought iron frames in new york","railroad rails made into i-beams"],"not":[],"definitions":[],"summary":"SUMMARY (not her words; no clear caption or slide line to quote): On her week-1 slide p.5 the first buildings with wrought-iron frames (1853-1858) are illustrated by the six-story Foundation Building in New York. The photo caption on the slide says it was a precursor of skeletal steel frame construction and the first building in New York to use structural wrought-iron I-beams, made from rolled railroad rails. (The caption names the person who made the beams; the name is left out here.)","answers":["What was new about the New York building of the 1850s on her history slide?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 1 slide 5 (photo captions, PARTLY); slide week 1 p.5","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1089,"term":"Braced versus unbraced frames: advantages and disadvantages (wk3 p.10)","topic":"Frames and bracing","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["compare a braced frame and an unbraced frame","advantages and disadvantages of braced and unbraced frames","braced frame versus moment frame","name two disadvantages of an unbraced frame","name two advantages of a braced frame","name two disadvantages of a braced frame","which lateral systems are pictured","x-braced frame shear wall moment frame","frame that sways or wobbles"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Produces localized stress concentrations at rigid joints","date":null},{"quote":"Requires care in the erection of connections in order to resist lateral loads properly","date":null},{"quote":"Accommodates service penetrations","date":null},{"quote":"Produces problems for a layout of windows and doors due to obstruction of bracings","date":null},{"quote":"Moment Frame = Unbraced","date":null},{"quote":"X-braced frame","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-3 slide 10. Unbraced (moment) frame, pictured as 'Moment Frame = Unbraced': advantage, flexibility for architectural design and layout; disadvantages, greater deflection and drift than braced frames or shear walls, localized stress concentrations at rigid joints, care in erecting connections to resist lateral loads, expensive moment connections. Braced frame (pictured as the X-braced frame): advantages, can be located inside or outside, accommodates service penetrations, can be located within partition walls, no moment connections; disadvantages, bracing obstructs windows and doors, fireproofing materials take up space, large gusset plates at beam-column connections. The third system pictured is the (steel plate) shear wall. In class (9/09) she added: the unbraced frame needs bigger members because it is flexible and will move.","answers":["Name two disadvantages of an unbraced (moment) frame.","Name two advantages and two disadvantages of a braced frame."],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 3 slide 10 (frame lists and pictured systems, PARTLY); slide week 3 p.10 and class 2026-09-09","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1090,"term":"First guess for a column: KL/r of about 50 (40 to 60)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what kl/r is a good first guess","first guess for column design","good place to start for a column design","kl/r about 50 to start","guesstimate kl/r for 10 to 15 feet","trial section slenderness","where do i start when designing a column"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"KL/r for L = 10' to 15' ~= 40 to 60 ksi -> a good place to start","date":null},{"quote":"A good place to start is a KL /R.","date":"2026-09-16"}],"summary":"Her week-4 slide 1 ('Guesstimate help'): for L = 10 to 15 ft, KL/r of about 40 to 60 is a good place to start (the slide prints 'ksi' after it, a slip: KL/r has no units); in class (9/16) she used KL/r of about 50, which gives phi Fcr = 37.5 ksi for Fy 50, then required Ag = Pu / (phi Fcr) and refined the size.","answers":["What KL/r is a good first guess for a column 10 to 15 ft long?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 4 slide 1 (first guess, PARTLY); slide week 4 p.1 and class 2026-09-16","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1091,"term":"A staggered path removes more than one hole but less than two","topic":"Tension members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["how much of the gross area does a staggered pattern remove","more than one hole but less than two","net area of a staggered path","check the straight path and the zigzag path","how many holes does the diagonal path cut","staggered bolt holes net area compared with one hole and two holes"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"This is a little bit more than one hole, but a little bit less than two holes.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"On 9/02 she compared the paths: the straight line through one hole removes one hole; the zigzag (staggered) path 'is a little bit more than one hole, but a little bit less than two holes', so you take into account the diagonal and add back s^2/4g, and you check every path to find the minimum net area.","answers":["A staggered pattern removes how much of the gross area, in holes?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 2 slide 7 (more than one, less than two, PARTLY); class 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1092,"term":"Minimum U for open shapes (Spec D3 on her slide)","topic":"Tension members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what is the least value u may take","minimum u for a w shape","u need not be less than the ratio of the connected element area to the gross area","lower limit of the shear lag factor","does the minimum u apply to hss or plates","spec d3 shear lag factor lower bound"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"the shear lag factor, U, need not be less than the ratio of the gross area of the connected element(s) to the member gross area.","date":null},{"quote":"This provision does not apply to closed sections, such as HSS, nor to plates.","date":null}],"summary":"Printed on her week-2 slide 2 (Spec D3): for open cross sections such as W, M, S, C or HP shapes, WTs, STs and single and double angles, the shear lag factor U need not be less than the ratio of the gross area of the connected element(s) to the member gross area; this does not apply to closed sections such as HSS, nor to plates.","answers":["For a W shape, what is the least value U may take?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 2 slide 2 (minimum U, NO); slide week 2 p.2","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1093,"term":"Welds in holes or slots: use the net area through the holes (Spec D2)","topic":"Tension members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["which net area is used when plug slot or fillet welds are used in holes","welds in holes or slots net area","equation d2-2 with welds in holes","plug weld slot weld net area","tensile rupture when welds are in holes"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Where connections use plug, slot, or fillet welds in holes or slots, the effective net area through the holes shall be used in Equation D2-2.","date":null}],"summary":"Printed on her week-2 slide 2 (Spec D2): where connections use plug, slot or fillet welds in holes or slots, the effective net area through the holes is used in the tensile rupture equation D2-2 (phi = 0.75 in LRFD).","answers":["Which net area goes into the rupture equation when plug, slot or fillet welds are used in holes or slots?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 2 slide 2 (welds in holes, NO); slide week 2 p.2","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1094,"term":"Tapered flange: thickness halfway between the edge and the web (Spec B4.3)","topic":"The Manual","kind":"fact","synonyms":["which flange thickness applies to a tapered flange","thickness of a tapered flange","nominal thickness halfway between the free edge and the web","flange thickness of a rolled shape","which thickness do i use for the flange of a w shape"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"For tapered flanges of rolled sections, the thickness is the nominal value halfway between the free edge and the corresponding face of the web.","date":null}],"summary":"Printed on her week-2 slide 7 (Spec B4.3): for tapered flanges of rolled sections, the thickness is the nominal value halfway between the free edge and the corresponding face of the web.","answers":["Which flange thickness applies to a rolled shape with tapered flanges?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 2 slide 7 (tapered flanges, NO); slide week 2 p.7","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1095,"term":"HSS design wall thickness: 0.93 times the nominal (Spec B4.3)","topic":"The Manual","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what design wall thickness is used for an hss","0.93 times the nominal wall thickness","design wall thickness of hss","hss made to a standard other than a1065 or a1085","which hss use the nominal wall thickness","why is the hss thickness reduced"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"For HSS produced according to other standards approved for use under this Specification, the design wall thickness, t, shall be taken equal to 0.93 times the nominal wall thickness.","date":null},{"quote":"The design wall thickness, t, shall be taken equal to the nominal thickness for box sections and HSS produced according to ASTM A1065/A1065M or ASTM A1085/A1085M.","date":null}],"summary":"Printed on her week-2 slide 7 (Spec B4.3): the design wall thickness t is used in calculations with the wall thickness of hollow structural sections; it is the nominal thickness for box sections and for HSS made to ASTM A1065 or A1085, and 0.93 times the nominal wall thickness for HSS made to other standards approved by the Specification.","answers":["What design wall thickness is used for an HSS made to a standard other than A1065 or A1085?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 2 slide 7 (HSS design thickness, NO); slide week 2 p.7","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1096,"term":"A pipe may be designed as a round HSS if it is A53 Grade B","topic":"The Manual","kind":"fact","synonyms":["when may a pipe be designed as a round hss","pipe designed as round hss","a53 grade b pipe","can i treat a pipe like a round hss","pipe and hss design rules"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"A pipe can be designed using the provisions of this Specification for round HSS as long as the pipe conforms to ASTM A53/A53M Grade B and the appropriate limitations of this Specification are used.","date":null}],"summary":"Printed on her week-2 slide 7 (Spec user note): a pipe can be designed using the Specification's provisions for round HSS as long as the pipe conforms to ASTM A53 Grade B and the appropriate limitations of the Specification are used.","answers":["When may a pipe be designed as a round HSS?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 2 slide 7 (pipe as HSS, NO); slide week 2 p.7","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1097,"term":"Net area of a slotted HSS welded to a gusset plate (Spec B4.3)","topic":"Tension members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["how is the net area of a slotted hss found","slotted hss welded to a gusset plate","net area of an hss with a slot","gross area minus the thickness times the width removed for the slot","hss with a slot cut for the gusset"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"For slotted HSS welded to a gusset plate, the net area, An, is the gross area minus the product of the thickness and the total width of material that is removed to form the slot.","date":null}],"summary":"Printed on her week-2 slide 7 (Spec B4.3): for a slotted HSS welded to a gusset plate, the net area An is the gross area minus the product of the thickness and the total width of material that is removed to form the slot.","answers":["How is the net area of a slotted HSS welded to a gusset plate found?"],"source":"D-slides-weeks-1-4.md week 2 slide 7 (slotted HSS, NO); slide week 2 p.7","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1098,"term":"Modulus of elasticity E: the stiffness of steel, 29,000 ksi for every grade","topic":"Stress-strain curve","kind":"fact","synonyms":["does higher strength steel have greater stiffness","does e change with the steel grade","is e the same for every steel","stiffness of steel","how stiff is steel","modulus of elasticity is a constant","e steel 29000 ksi","slope of the straight line of the stress strain diagram","stiffness versus strength","what is the stiffness of a stronger steel"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Proportional Limit: Stress/Strain diagram is linear up to this point; the slope of the straight-line E= Modulus of elasticity","date":null},{"quote":"E steel = 29,000 ksi a constant","date":null}],"summary":"Her week-1 slide p.3: the slope of the straight line of the stress-strain diagram is E, the modulus of elasticity (the stiffness), and E for steel is 29,000 ksi, a constant. Strength (Fy, Fu) changes from grade to grade (A36 36/58, A992 50/65) but E does not: her slides use the same 29,000 ksi for Fy 50, 46 and 36 when they work out the elastic/inelastic boundary KL/r (113, 118, 134). Her Quiz 1 key words the definition as: in the elastic range, the ratio of stress to strain.","answers":["Does higher-strength steel have a greater modulus of elasticity (stiffness)?","What is E for steel, and does it depend on the grade?"],"source":"council O6 cards (OpenAI 1, xAI 3, Google 1) kept in her words; slide week 1 p.3","tier":"on-her-slides"},{"n":1099,"term":"No bolt holes (everything welded): check yielding only; there is no rupture check","topic":"Tension members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["welded connection which checks","no bolt holes which limit state","do i check rupture if there are no holes","all welded tension member","no holes means yielding only","fracture check not needed when welded","tear check not needed","only check yielding on the gross area","welded all around which checks"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"If there are no bolt holes at all, then we only check zone yielding.","date":"2026-09-30"},{"quote":"If there's no holes or welded, we don't worry about rupture because there are no holes.","date":"2026-09-30"},{"quote":"If you're using welds there's no bolts.","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"If there are no bolt holes at all (all the connections are welded), you only check yielding on the gross area, factored load divided by the strength of the material (Ag >= Pu / (0.9 Fy)); you do not worry about rupture because there are no holes. (The 9/30 caption says 'zone yielding', read as yielding.) With welds there are no bolts, so no holes and no net-area reduction.","answers":["Welded all around, or no holes: which tension checks do you make?","Do you check rupture on a welded tension member with no holes?"],"source":"council O6 card (xAI 1) supported by C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md inventory #21, #72 and A-classes-0826-0902.md item 173; classes 2026-09-30 and 2026-09-02","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1100,"term":"A load not labelled factored is unfactored (service or working load)","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"fact","synonyms":["how do i know whether a load is factored","load not marked factored","if nothing is said the load is unfactored","service load unfactored","working load unfactored","loads from the code are unfactored","do i factor the given loads","ultimate or factored means already factored","snow load unfactored"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"It doesn't have to be labelled working stress. If it's not labelled ultimate or factored, it's unfactored.","date":"2026-09-02"},{"quote":"If they say nothing, it means it's unfactored.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"We open up a code and looking at the snow load or water retention loads, they are going to be unfactored because they are going to reflect the usage.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"A load does not have to be labelled working or service: if it is not labelled ultimate or factored, it is unfactored, and you apply the load factors yourself (1.2 D + 1.6 L). If a load is labelled ultimate (or Pu, factored) it is already factored: do not factor it again. Loads you open up in a code, such as snow or water retention loads, are unfactored because they reflect the usage; loads on a set of drawings are unfactored.","answers":["A load is not marked factored; what is it, and what do you do with it?"],"source":"council O6 card (xAI 2); A-classes-0826-0902.md item 182; B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md items B-026, B-150; classes 2026-09-02, 2026-09-09 and 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1101,"term":"Fire and steel: loses strength; a two-hour rating is two hours to evacuate","topic":"Construction and fire","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what is the fire danger to steel","what is a two hour fire rating","two hours to evacuate","steel loses strength in a fire","ribbon candy","steel is an excellent conductor of heat","why fireproof steel","fire rating means time to leave the building","does steel melt in a fire","why is fireproofing needed"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Steel is an excellent conductor of heat.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"It isn't because the steel melted, it' s because at high temperatures.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"It isn't to save the building, it's to make sure people can evacuate before the building collapses.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"So when you go to the code and it says something like all office buildings have to have a two hour fire rate. It means you have two hours to evacuate the building.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"One of the bad things about steel is when it's exposed to high temperatures. It really just looses it's strength and becomes ribbon candy.","date":"2026-09-23"}],"summary":"Her words: steel is an excellent conductor of heat; in a fire it looks like ribbon candy, not because it melted but because at high temperatures it loses its strength. Fireproofing is not there to save the building but to make sure people can evacuate before the building collapses; when the code says all office buildings need a two-hour fire rating it means two hours to evacuate. On 9/23 she repeated that steel exposed to high temperatures loses its strength and becomes ribbon candy, adding that she imagines it probably does melt too. (This entry stores her version only.)","answers":["What is the fire danger to steel, and what does a two-hour fire rating mean?"],"source":"council O6 card (xAI 4); A-classes-0826-0902.md items 83-86; B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md item B-181; classes 2026-08-26 and 2026-09-23","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1102,"term":"U for a tee (WT): same as a wide flange, with the tee's own depth","topic":"Tension members","kind":"fact","synonyms":["what is u for a tee","u for a wt shape","shear lag factor of a structural tee","wt treated like a wide flange","which depth do i use for a wt","wt7x19 u 0.9","case 7 tees cut from shapes","tee connected through the flange u"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"WT's are treated the same way Y flanges are.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"Case 7 has to do with white flange s, M. All double shapes. Also T's cut from shapes.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"Applies to W, M, S, HP, WT shapes: If we are connected only through the flanges","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"Case 7 of the shear lag table covers W, M, S, HP shapes and also tees cut from shapes: 'WTs are treated the same way wide flanges are'. If only the web is connected U = 0.70; if connected through the flanges, compare the flange width bf with 2/3 of the depth d (bf at least 2/3 d gives U = 0.90, narrower gives 0.85). In her WT7x19 example (9/02) bf = 6.77 against 2/3 of 7.05 (the tee's own depth), so U = 0.9 and the capacity came out about 205 k.","answers":["What is the shear lag factor for a tee bolted through its flange, and which depth do you compare with?"],"source":"council O6 card (xAI 6) supported by A-classes-0826-0902.md item 184 and B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md items B-013, B-022; classes 2026-09-02 and 2026-09-09","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1103,"term":"Why the Manual's column table gives less than Euler (safety factors)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why is the table value lower than euler","what is missing in euler that is in the hss table","we don't design for imminent failure","robust safety factors because we design for human life","limitations of euler as a design equation","euler gives 55 k the table gives 43.3 k","why not use euler to design","what does the manual table add to euler"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"We don't design for imminent failure. We have very robust safety factors because we design for human life.","date":"2026-09-30"},{"quote":"NOTE limitations of Euler equation as a design equation:","date":null}],"summary":"In her round-HSS sample (HSS 7 x 0.25, 32 ft) the Euler load came out about 55 k while the Manual's column table gives 43.3 k. What is missing in Euler that the table has is safety factors: 'We don't design for imminent failure. We have very robust safety factors because we design for human life.' Her slide lists the limitations of the Euler equation as a design equation: no safety factor, independent of material strength, assumed pin ends, valid only in the elastic range.","answers":["What is missing in Euler that is in the HSS column tables?","Why is the Manual's column capacity lower than the Euler load?"],"source":"council O6 card (xAI 10); C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md inventory #86 and G10; class 2026-09-30 and slide week 3 p.2","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1104,"term":"Why the hole is the bolt diameter plus 1/8 in (1/16 + 1/16)","topic":"Tension members","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why add 1/8 inch to the bolt diameter","hole is bolt plus an eighth","why is the hole bigger than the bolt","1/16 clearance plus 1/16 damage","the process of making the hole damages the material","hole size for a 3/4 inch bolt","7/8 inch hole for a 3/4 inch bolt","punched hole damages the steel","how big is a bolt hole"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Tension in the bolt hole is actually the bolt diameter plus an eighth of an inch. Why? It's manufactured to be a 16th inch of a diameter.","date":"2026-09-02"},{"quote":"We also subject an additional 16 th of an inch because the process for making these holes can damage the materials.","date":"2026-09-02"},{"quote":"The hole is an eighth of an inch larger. 16th of an inch because the process of making the bolt hold the material.","date":"2026-09-30"}],"summary":"The hole whose area you deduct is the bolt diameter plus an eighth of an inch: it is manufactured a 16th of an inch larger than the bolt, and an additional 16th of an inch is allowed because the process of making the holes can damage the material (a 3/4 in bolt gives a 7/8 in hole). The area taken out is a rectangle: this full hole width times the thickness of the element. She repeated the 1/8 in rule among the midterm topics on 9/30 and said she still wants the class to understand how bolts work.","answers":["Why is the hole bolt diameter + 1/8 in?","A drawing gives 3/4 in bolts; what hole size do you use in the net area?"],"source":"council O6 cards (Google 2, DeepSeek 1); A-classes-0826-0902.md items 132-134; C-review-0930-and-weeks-5-6.md inventory #27; classes 2026-09-02 and 2026-09-30","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1105,"term":"Why the live-load factor 1.6 is bigger than the dead-load factor 1.2","topic":"Loads and LRFD","kind":"reason","synonyms":["why is 1.6 bigger than 1.2","why is the live load factor larger than the dead load factor","dead load is more accurate than live load","why different load factors for dead and live","live load is harder to predict","apartment turned fraternity","50 year storms","why a bigger safety factor on live load","why not put the safety factor on the material side"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Live load is harder to grasp. It was an apartment now a fraternity. Have parties every night.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"We can be very accurate with dead load and less accurate with live loads. So that's a bigger safety factor.","date":"2026-08-26"}],"summary":"She can estimate dead load pretty accurately if the architect and the designs are good; live load is harder to grasp (an apartment that becomes a fraternity with parties every night; 50-year storms). So the live load needs the bigger safety factor: 1.2 on dead load and 1.6 on live load. Putting one safety factor on the material side says the two loads are equally uncertain, which she said is not what we feel today: we can be very accurate with dead load and less accurate with live loads.","answers":["Why is the live-load factor larger than the dead-load factor?","Why compare load combinations instead of one factor on the material side?"],"source":"council O6 card (DeepSeek 3); A-classes-0826-0902.md items 108, 114; class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1106,"term":"Strain is how much a member stretches: the change in length, designated epsilon","topic":"Stress-strain curve","kind":"definition","synonyms":["stretch","how much does it stretch","elongation","how much does the steel elongate","change in length","deformation per unit length","strain epsilon","it's going to want to grow a little bit","what is the physical response to the load"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Tension forces. It's going to want to grow a little bit.","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"Strain: Is it equal to the change in length, designated epsilon?","date":"2026-08-26"},{"quote":"Applied forces -> Physical change (change in length or shape)","date":null},{"quote":"Steel subject to an applied Tension Force P will elongate Delta","date":null}],"summary":"When you put a load on a structure there is a physical change: in tension it is going to want to grow a little bit (stretch). That physical response is strain, the change in length (relative to the original length, delta over L), designated epsilon. Her week-1 slide: applied forces give a physical change (change in length or shape); steel under a tension force P will elongate by delta, and strain is epsilon = delta / L, plotted against stress f = P/A. She pictured it in tension because it is easier to visualize.","answers":["What is strain, and which Greek letter stands for it?","What is the physical response of a member to a load?"],"source":"A-classes-0826-0902.md 2A 'Strain symbol' (PARTLY); everyday-word synonyms requested by the coordinator; slide week 1 p.2 and class 2026-08-26","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1107,"term":"A short column crushes (squashes)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"definition","synonyms":["squash","squashes","crush","crushes","a short column fails by squashing","yielding of a column","a column that yields in compression","material strength is exhausted","what does a short column do","inelastic failure of a column"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Short columns crushed and failing. We exceed the maximum low it can resist and the material strengths is exhausted and squashed.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"If we exhaust the strengths of the steel, it crushes.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"Short Columns fail by crushing","date":null}],"summary":"Short columns crush: the maximum load the material can resist is exceeded, the strength of the steel is exhausted and the column squashes (her slide calls it an inelastic failure: material strength is exhausted). Long columns do not crush; they buckle.","answers":["How does a short column fail?","What is crushing in a compression member?"],"source":"everyday-word synonyms requested by the coordinator; B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md items B-032, B-033; class 2026-09-09 and slide week 3 p.1","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1108,"term":"A long column buckles (bows sideways)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"definition","synonyms":["bow","bows sideways","bend sideways","buckle","what is buckling","a long column fails by bowing out","becomes unstable","the more spaghetti like direction","which way does a column buckle"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"And if it becomes geometrically unstable, it buckles.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"Long columns fail and buckle. They become unstable about the weaker of two axis.","date":"2026-09-09"},{"quote":"It'll buckle first about the axis with the smaller r.","date":"2026-09-16"}],"summary":"If a compression member becomes geometrically unstable it buckles: long columns buckle, becoming unstable about the weaker of the two axes, and elastic buckling happens well before the strength of the steel is exhausted. A column starts to deform (curve), and it buckles first about the axis with the smaller r, the more 'spaghetti-like' (more slender) direction.","answers":["How does a long column fail?","About which axis does a column buckle first?"],"source":"everyday-word synonyms requested by the coordinator; B-classes-0909-0916-0923.md items B-032, B-034, B-061; classes 2026-09-09 and 2026-09-16","tier":"said-in-class"},{"n":1109,"term":"Ductile steel stretches before it fails; brittle material fails suddenly","topic":"Steel as a material","kind":"comparison","synonyms":["ductile","what does ductile mean in plain words","stretches a lot before it fails","brittle","snaps suddenly","sudden failure","fails abruptly","ductile steel versus brittle concrete","large deformations without failure"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Ductile (withstands large deformations w/o failure)","date":null},{"quote":"(vs a brittle material such as concrete -> sudden failure)","date":null},{"quote":"it fails abruptly because it's brittle","date":"2026-09-02"}],"summary":"Her slides: steel is ductile, it withstands large deformations without failure (the plastic range gives local deformations before failure and lets stresses be redistributed), against a brittle material such as concrete, which fails suddenly. In class (8/26) she described the yielding steel as having a lot of deformation, 'very, very saggy'; on 9/02 she said that near its ultimate strength steel fails abruptly because it is brittle.","answers":["What is the difference between a ductile and a brittle material?"],"source":"everyday-word synonyms requested by the coordinator; slide week 1 p.3 and p.8; classes 2026-08-26 and 2026-09-02","tier":"on-her-slides"}]; })(typeof window !== "undefined" ? window : (typeof globalThis !== "undefined" ? globalThis : this));

